(window.I18N_T = window.I18N_T || {}).en = ["Chapter 1  Kimbap with Burst Sides", "Three months since I stamped the divorce papers. My wife's heart had gone to someone else long before that.", "Outside the courthouse, she never once brought up Seoyun. I couldn't tell if I was grateful or hurt.", "What was left: me, a thirty-four-year-old game programmer, and Seoyun, seven.", "Eleven at night. On the laptop on the kitchen table, the build due tomorrow morning was spitting out its third error.", "Yesterday's dishes in the sink. Three days of clothes in the laundry basket.", "In the fridge: expired milk, and the stickers Seoyun had stuck on the door.", "(It's fine. I can do this. Everyone gets by like this.)", "Even as I muttered it, the red error messages kept growing, one line at a time.", "Dad.", "I turned around. Seoyun was standing in the doorway in her pajamas, clutching her toy rabbit by the ear.", "Why aren't you asleep? Bad dream?", "No. I have a question.", "Dad, is Mom never coming back?", "(Here it is. The question I've been putting off for three months.)", "Is Mom never coming back? What do I tell her?", "“No. Mom lives in a different home now. But she's still your mom. That won't change.”", "…Then what about you? Are you going somewhere too?", "I'm not going anywhere. Not ever.", "Seoyun looked at me for a long time, then nodded once.", "Okay then.", "“…Mom went somewhere far away for work.”", "Liar. I heard Grandma on the phone.", "You're not going, right?", "…I'm not. I'm sorry. Dad lied.", "Don't do it again. I'm seven.", "Seoyun climbed onto a kitchen chair and peered at my laptop screen.", "Lots of red words. Are you in trouble?", "Yeah. The computer is scolding me.", "Bad computer.", "For the first time in three months, I laughed.", "Dad, tomorrow's the field trip… can you make me kimbap?", "(Kimbap. I've never rolled one in my life. And the build is due tomorrow morning.)", "The other kids' moms make theirs. But I have you.", "I've never rolled a single kimbap.", "“I'll make you the best kimbap ever.”", "I put Seoyun to bed and ran to the convenience store. Seaweed, pickled radish, ham. The cashier smiled at my one a.m. shopping basket.", "Four in the morning. Seven rolls of kimbap with burst sides piled up. Only on the eighth did one finally hold its shape.", "“Can we just buy some? Dad's not very good at this.”", "…Okay. It's fine.", "That “it's fine” face haunted me all night. While the build ran, I ended up running to the convenience store anyway.", "Four in the morning. Seven rolls of kimbap with burst sides piled up. Only on the eighth did one finally hold its shape.", "I ate the burst ones and packed the good roll into her lunch box. I closed the lid, then opened it again and tucked one burst piece into the corner.", "(There should be some proof that Dad made it.)", "I was ten minutes late to work that day, and the build didn't go up until lunch.", "My team lead sighed, but strangely, I had no regrets.", "That evening Seoyun ran in, shaking her empty lunch box.", "Your kimbap was the biggest! Everyone wanted a bite.", "But one of them was burst.", "That's… Dad's signature.", "Signature? Then put it in every time.", "From then on, there was always one burst piece in Seoyun's lunch. Some days I burst it on purpose.", "On weekends we went to my parents' house. Some days just wouldn't roll on my own.", "Your face has shrunk to half. Are you eating?", "I am. Seven rolls of kimbap at a time.", "That's not food, that's failure.", "We'll watch the girl. Get some sleep. Try to carry it all alone and your back goes first.", "I'm sorry. A grown man, still holding out his hand.", "A parent's hands are there to be held out to.", "A grown man holding out his hand again.", "“Thank you. I'll lean on you for a while.”", "Good. That's what I wanted to hear.", "Leaning on people is a skill too. You learned it too late.", "“I'll stand on my own soon.”", "You can take your time standing on your own. Just don't fall.", "Who did he get that stubbornness from.", "The barley tea Mother brought out was nutty even after it cooled. Ever since I was a boy, the water in this house was always barley tea.", "I lay down on the porch floor, and when I opened my eyes the sun was low. The first dreamless sleep in three months.", "Seoyun was tiptoeing around so she wouldn't wake you.", "Seoyun was lying on her stomach on the porch, drawing. A big hand holding a small hand.", "This is your hand. I drew the wrist brace too.", "Why is the hand so big?", "Your hand is big. Mine fits all the way inside.", "Seoyun laid her hand on my palm to show me. It really did fit all the way.", "She does nothing but draw these days. She won't talk.", "She won't talk?", "Same at kindergarten, they say. When anyone mentions her mom, she shuts her mouth and grabs a crayon.", "Something in my chest sank. Instead of crying, she was drawing.", "Instead of crying, she's drawing.", "(Lie down beside Seoyun and draw with her)", "I picked up a crayon and drew a wobbly rabbit next to the hand.", "Dad, is that a pig?", "It's a rabbit.", "Dad, don't draw. I'll draw. You stay next to me.", "Stay next to me. That was all she wanted.", "“Seoyun, if you're sad, you can say you're sad.”", "I'm not sad.", "She gripped the crayon so hard the paper tore a little.", "…Just a little sad. Drawing makes it okay.", "Then draw lots. I'll buy you all the paper you want.", "On the way home, Seoyun stopped in front of a stationery shop. There was a yellow hairpin in the window.", "Mom said she'd buy me that.", "I went in without a word and bought the hairpin. Three thousand won.", "With clumsy hands I clipped it into her bangs. It was crooked, but Seoyun didn't fix it.", "You bought it, so it's yours. Something of yours, in my hair.", "For the next thirteen years, every time that hairpin faded, I would buy the same one again.", "And the drawing of the hands she made that day would hang beside my monitor and pull all-nighters with me for fourteen years.", "Chapter 2  Dead-Last Dad", "Seoyun started elementary school. Her backpack was bigger than her back.", "On entrance day, the other kids held their moms' hands for photos. Seoyun held my wrist brace.", "Your hand gets sweaty in pictures. I like this better.", "Her small hand gripped the Velcro of the brace until her knuckles went white. My jaw ached from holding a smile.", "The problem was after school. Afterschool care ended at five, and I got off work at eight at the earliest.", "My parents took turns on weekdays, but Saturday shifts had no answer.", "So every Saturday, Seoyun came to work with me.", "Why does your office always have snacks?", "Without snacks, the guys here won't work.", "Seoyun set up camp on the floor beside my chair. A sketchbook, crayons, and a juice box were all her belongings.", "While I typed code, she drew. The monitor, the keyboard, the cold coffee, and my back.", "Dad, what are you writing on that black screen?", "Letters to the game. “Please move like this.”", "Does the game listen?", "No. It listens worse than you.", "I listen well.", "Just then my team lead walked by and stopped. I tensed up. He couldn't possibly like me bringing a kid.", "Mr. Han, is this a daycare now?", "The team lead is looking down at the sketchbook.", "“I'm sorry. I had no one to leave her with. My work won't suffer.”", "The team lead stared down at Seoyun's sketchbook for a long time.", "…Is this me? The belly's a bit big.", "It really is big.", "The office went quiet, and then the team lead burst out laughing first.", "“This is my daughter. On Saturdays, she comes to work with me.”", "I surprised myself. It was the first time I'd said it without an excuse.", "…Bold. And who's in the drawing?", "You, mister. Your belly's a bit big.", "The office went quiet, and then the team lead burst out laughing first.", "Kid, next week draw me handsome. Then you get a whole box of snacks.", "From that day, Seoyun was the studio's Saturday employee. She was paid in snacks.", "The designers handed down their old markers, and Seoyun learned the word “character” for the first time.", "Dad, is a character a fake person?", "Fake, but the kind of person people end up loving like they're real.", "Then I'll make one. A fake person people love.", "That day Seoyun wrote “Character No. 1” on the first page of her sketchbook and drew a man with glasses. There was a black band on his wrist.", "In a house without a mom, everything a mom used to do is left behind. I discovered those things one by one.", "The first discovery was hair.", "In the spring of second grade, Seoyun stood in front of the mirror for a long time.", "Dad, I want my hair tied too. Jiho comes in two braids every day.", "(Braids. …How does that structure even work.)", "Braiding hair. I've never done it.", "“Let's try. Dad will learn.”", "That night, after Seoyun fell asleep, I tied three LAN cables to a table leg and practiced braiding.", "By two a.m., the LAN cables were in a perfect three-strand braid. The problem was that human hair is far slipperier than LAN cable.", "“Dad's hands can't do that. Want to go to a hair salon?”", "We can't go to the salon every day. …It's fine. I'll just leave it down.", "That “it's fine” again. That night, I tied three LAN cables to a table leg and practiced braiding.", "By two a.m., the LAN cables were in a perfect three-strand braid. The problem was that human hair is far slipperier than LAN cable.", "The next morning, Seoyun sat on a kitchen chair and I stood behind her. Armed with a comb, hair ties, and grim resolve.", "Ow, Dad!", "Sorry. Again.", "Dad, it's crooked!", "That's… the design.", "Twenty minutes later, Seoyun's head held something that couldn't quite be called pigtails; the two sides differed by a hand's width.", "Seoyun stared into the mirror for a long time. I held my breath.", "…One side's a bunny and one side's a puppy.", "Should I undo it?", "No. I'll go like this. It's the first one you ever tied.", "That day Seoyun went to school with lopsided hair. The yellow hairpin was clipped on the higher side.", "A month later the sides matched. Three months later I could do fishtail braids. For a while my nickname at work was “the LAN Cable Hairdresser.”", "The second discovery was school supplies.", "There was a thing called the notice book. Every day it came home with coded messages. “Colored paper, glue, 2 yogurt bottles, a childhood photo of a parent.”", "Finding out at eleven at night that we needed yogurt bottles happened about twice a week.", "That day was the same. At the office on Saturday, Seoyun held out her notice book.", "Dad, I have to make an “Our Family Newspaper” by Monday. With photos and articles.", "Articles?", "You write about what happened at our house.", "I took my eyes off the monitor and sat on the floor next to Seoyun. I let the build run.", "What's the latest news at our house?", "Dad burned the rolled omelet.", "Let's leave that out.", "Dad practiced braiding with LAN cables.", "That's also… no, let's put that in. It's a scoop.", "We made the newspaper on the office floor. Seoyun named it: The Bunny and Puppy Daily.", "The front-page story: “Han Jeongwoo (35) Masters Braiding With LAN Cables… Daughter: ‘One Side's a Bunny.’” Instead of a photo, there was Seoyun's drawing.", "A designer passing by touched up the masthead lettering, and a planner drew four-panel comic boxes.", "On Monday, The Bunny and Puppy Daily went up in the middle of the board at the back of the classroom. The homeroom teacher took a photo and sent it to me.", "A single photo from her homeroom teacher.", "(Make the photo my phone wallpaper)", "That photo stayed my phone wallpaper for the next four years. Someone asked about it at every meeting, and I explained every time.", "(Send the photo to my parents)", "(text) Why is it called Bunny and Puppy.", "(reply) Seoyun named it.", "(text) The child's better than you.", "The summer vacation of third grade. Seoyun was doing her vacation homework on the porch at my parents' house.", "The homework was “Introduce Your Family.”", "Grandma, does a family have to have a mom and a dad?", "Who said that?", "The homework paper has boxes like that. A dad box and a mom box.", "Mother looked at me. I couldn't find an answer.", "If the boxes are wrong, fix the boxes. Paper should fit people, not people fit paper.", "Seoyun thought for a long time, then drew a line through the mom box and wrote over it in big letters.", "“Dad (a double serving).”", "…What's a double serving?", "You do the work of two people. You make twice the kimbap too.", "I should have laughed, but my throat closed up. I turned away, pretending to drink barley tea.", "Honestly, can't you even cry properly in front of the child?", "I didn't cry.", "You did. Your ears turned red. When Dad cries, his ears go red first.", "The girl who had been drawing me since she was seven knew my face better than I did.", "Autumn of third grade. Sports day.", "The school notice read: “Parents' race: we hope many fathers will take part.”", "Dad, you're running, right? Jiho's dad came in first last year.", "(When did I last run. Other than chasing a bus.)", "When did I last run?", "“Of course. Dad used to be quite the runner.”", "A lie. I couldn't run back then either.", "Really? Then I'll make a cheering sign!", "“Can Dad just cheer instead…”", "…Okay. It's fine.", "That “it's fine” again. It's the phrase I can stand least in the world.", "No. I'm running. Even if I come in last, I'm running.", "Really? Then I'll make a cheering sign!", "I stood at the starting line. The fathers on either side were retying their sneakers. I was in dress shoes.", "The gunshot.", "Ten meters in, I knew. This wasn't going to work. At thirty meters I was gasping, and at fifty my wrist brace came loose and flew off.", "The other fathers' backs grew smaller and smaller.", "Then Seoyun's voice split the field.", "Dad! It's okay to be last! Just don't fall!", "Her sign said, in crayon: “My Dad = Character No. 1.”", "I burst out laughing and couldn't run anymore. Laughing, panting, I came in an overwhelming last.", "At the finish line Seoyun ran up and threw her arms around my waist.", "You ran the longest. So you ran the most.", "That's… some strange math.", "It's my math.", "The next week, Seoyun drew “My Dad Running Dead Last” and won a prize in the school art contest.", "Holding the certificate, her homeroom teacher spoke to me quietly.", "Mr. Han, Seoyun doesn't say much, but her drawings have a lot to say.", "And everything they say is about you.", "The third discovery was birthdays.", "Seoyun's ninth birthday. She'd circled the date on the calendar but didn't say a word.", "That night, from under the blanket, came a barely audible voice.", "Dad… moms are the ones who throw birthday parties, right?", "Who said that?", "Jiho and Minseo both had their moms do it. At home. With balloons.", "That night I searched “how to throw a kids' birthday party.” Four million results. More daunting than bug hunting.", "Balloons, party hats, tteokbokki, cake. And “contact the other mothers in advance.”", "(The mothers. …That group chat. The group chat everyone's in but me.)", "The group chat everyone's in but me. Should I knock first?", "(Gather my courage and call Jiho's mom)", "I found the number in the emergency contact list. My hands sweated while it rang. I was more nervous than at a job interview.", "Oh, Seoyun's dad! We were just about to call you. What can we help with?", "Ten minutes later, I'd been invited to the group chat. Twelve mothers and one father.", "(Prepare everything on my own)", "I printed the invitations myself. The font looked like a company report.", "The next day after school, Jiho's mom came up waving the invitation.", "Seoyun's dad, you're doing it all yourself? I'll bring the tteokbokki. And join the group chat.", "And so I was invited into a group chat of twelve mothers and one father.", "Saturday. Fifty balloons I'd blown up all night rolled around the living room. My cheeks were numb.", "I didn't make the cake myself. After the omelet incident, I knew my limits.", "Eight kids came. Within thirty minutes the living room was a battlefield.", "I had to host. What I prepared was very much a game-company employee's idea.", "All right, we're going on a treasure hunt. The clue's on this paper. Crack the code and you'll find the next spot.", "The kids went wild. The code was the times tables, and the final treasure was the ice cream in the fridge.", "I heard Jiho whisper to Seoyun.", "Your dad's awesome. My mom can't make codes.", "Instead of answering, Seoyun shrugged. But those shoulders were the highest I'd ever seen them.", "After the kids left, Seoyun lay down in the pile of balloons.", "Dad, it wasn't moms who throw them after all. Birthday parties.", "Yeah. Whoever's willing throws them.", "Then I'll throw yours. I'll make codes too.", "Three months later, my birthday. There was a note on the table. “Code: 3×4 = ? Check that drawer.”", "We didn't have a twelfth drawer. After half an hour of searching, I found the present inside the fourth sock in the third drawer.", "A coupon drawn in crayon. “10 shoulder rubs. Valid until Dad becomes a grandpa.”", "Seven of those coupons are still left.", "That winter Seoyun caught the flu. Her fever climbed to thirty-nine degrees.", "Outside, something that was neither snow nor rain scraped down the window all night. Sleet.", "Of all weeks, it was one week before launch. A week I couldn't take off.", "I set up my laptop on the kitchen table, typing code with one hand and changing the wet towel with the other.", "Dad… you have to go to work.", "Work came home. See, it's right here.", "Will you get in trouble?", "Getting in trouble is Dad's job. The computer scolds me, and my boss scolds me.", "…I don't want you to get in trouble because of me.", "My feverish child is worrying about me.", "“Getting in trouble because of you doesn't hurt one bit.”", "Liar.", "It's true. The only time Dad hurts is when you're sick.", "Seoyun rested her burning forehead on the back of my hand. It felt like the fever was moving into my hand.", "“Grown-ups just get scolded a bit. That's life.”", "Then I won't grow up.", "You have to. You have to grow up to be someone who draws characters.", "…Then I'll be a grown-up who only gets scolded a little.", "At three a.m. the fever broke. Seoyun mumbled in her sleep.", "Dad, did you eat…?", "She was the sick one. Only then did I realize I'd skipped dinner.", "After that, those words became our family's greeting. Instead of “sleep well,” instead of “I love you.”", "“Dad, did you eat?” “Did you, Seoyun?”", "The day spring came and she started fourth grade, on the porch at my parents' house, Seoyun asked me for something for the first time.", "Dad, I want to go to art school. I really want to learn.", "I calculated the tuition. Tight. Very tight.", "Go. Dad will figure it out.", "“Dad will figure it out.” I didn't know then how many years of weekends those words would take.", "Chapter 3  To All Mothers", "The fourth discovery was that a child keeps things hidden, all alone.", "Spring of fourth grade, a Tuesday in April. In the middle of a meeting, an unknown number called.", "Is this Seoyun's father? This is the school. Seoyun cut her finger a little with a knife. It's not serious, but if you can come, we'd appreciate it.", "(A knife? At an elementary school?)", "My mind went blank. I ran out of the meeting room with my laptop still open.", "At the school, I was led not to the nurse's office but to the cooking classroom. Through the hallway window I could see people in aprons.", "Mothers. At every counter, one mom and one child. The sound of cucumbers being sliced, and laughter.", "And at the last counter by the window, Seoyun stood alone. A bandage around her index finger, looking down at a half-sliced cucumber.", "Oh, you're here. Today's the parent participation class. You got the notice, didn't you?", "…No. I didn't.", "Something flickered across the teacher's face. She glanced at Seoyun once and lowered her voice.", "Seoyun said her mother was too busy to come. I was going to be her partner, but while I stepped away to help another group, she was cutting by herself and…", "Her mom was busy. That's what Seoyun had said. Not that she didn't have a mom.", "When I opened the door, the classroom went quiet for a moment. A man with a company ID around his neck and no apron. Every mother's eyes turned to me at once.", "Seoyun looked up. The moment she saw me, her face went pale. It wasn't a happy face.", "…Why did you come?", "The boy at the next counter said loudly. No malice in it, which made it hurt more.", "Huh? I thought her mom was busy. Her dad came? Don't you have a mom?", "The whole classroom held its breath. Seoyun froze with the cucumber in her hand. The bandaged finger was white.", "The whole classroom is watching us.", "(Go stand beside Seoyun) “Dad came instead. Let's do it together today.”", "I borrowed a spare apron from the teacher. It was a child's size and only reached above my knees.", "I stood beside Seoyun. She didn't look at me, but she slid the knife toward me.", "(Go over to the boy and try to explain)", "I started to say something to the boy, then stopped. Seoyun was looking at me, shaking her head ever so slightly. Her face said, please.", "I closed my mouth and borrowed a spare apron from the teacher. It was a child's size and only reached above my knees. I stood beside Seoyun.", "The menu was cucumber sandwiches and rice balls. I was confident with a knife; ten years of living alone. But the rice balls kept falling apart in my hands.", "A mother at the next counter who'd been sneaking glances handed me some plastic wrap. Jiho's mom. The one from the birthday group chat.", "Wrap it in this and squeeze, and it won't fall apart. Seoyun, your dad's doing great.", "Seoyun didn't answer. She didn't smile once until the class ended. I couldn't smile either.", "The other kids left the school gate holding their moms' hands. Seoyun walked holding my ID lanyard instead of my hand. Without a word.", "That night, sorting out her backpack, I found a crumpled paper at the very bottom.", "“4th Grade Parent Participation Class. Mothers are asked to bring an apron.”", "Over the word “Mothers” there were pencil lines, again and again. Enough to tear the paper.", "Seoyun. Why didn't you show this to me?", "…", "If I'd known, I would've taken the day off. Then you wouldn't have cut yourself alone.", "It's weirder if you come!", "Seoyun shouted. The first time. That loud.", "Everyone comes with their mom. If you come, everyone knows I don't have one. That's why I said she was busy…", "When you came today, everyone stared. Jihun asked out loud. That's why I didn't show you!", "Crying, she snatched the paper and crumpled it again.", "Why doesn't our house have a mom? All the other kids have one. They even bring their mom's apron.", "I couldn't say anything. For four years I'd made kimbap, braided hair, blown up balloons. I thought I could do all of it.", "But no matter what I did, the word “Mothers” on the paper wouldn't erase. And in front of that word, every day, my child had been lying alone.", "In front of the word “Mothers.”", "(Kneel to meet her eyes) “I'm sorry. Dad can't be a mom.”", "…I know.", "But Seoyun, you don't have to lie that she's busy. Not having a mom isn't your fault. It's nothing to hide.", "Seoyun buried her face in my shoulder and cried for a long time. Patting that small back, I cried too.", "“Dad will do Mom's share too. Don't worry.”", "You can't. You're Dad.", "She was right. A promise to do everything might be the very thing that left her more alone.", "…Right. Dad can't be a mom. But Seoyun, you don't have to lie that she's busy. It's not your fault.", "Seoyun buried her face in my shoulder and cried for a long time. Patting that small back, I cried too.", "Dad. Next time there's something like that… just come. Even if it's weird.", "Okay. I'll come weird. The weirdest.", "…Don't do that.", "That night I smoothed out the notice she'd crumpled twice and ironed it. I didn't throw it away. It was a day my child had endured alone.", "The fifth discovery was that I couldn't discover everything.", "Summer vacation of fourth grade. At my parents' house, Seoyun went to the public bathhouse with my mother.", "She came back with wet hair and sat on the porch drinking a yogurt drink. Quieter than usual.", "Jeongwoo, come here a moment.", "In the kitchen, Mother lowered her voice.", "She asked me in the bath. Whether Grandma had a mom. Whether her mom scrubbed her back.", "I said she did. Then she asked me to scrub hers too. I scrubbed for a long time.", "There are things you can't give her. That's not your fault. It's just how it is. Those things, I'll do.", "There are things I can't give her.", "“…I wanted to do everything for her myself.”", "Wanting to do everything is greed. A child is better off with many hands.", "I know you're working hard doing two people's share alone. But the third and fourth shares, let's split.", "“Thank you. I'll leave that to you, Mother.”", "Leave it to me, nothing. She's my granddaughter.", "You're finally learning to lean. I thought it'd take ten years; it took four.", "When I went back to the porch, Seoyun held out a yogurt bottle.", "Dad, Grandma's the best at scrubbing backs. But you're the best at tying hair.", "That's thanks to the LAN cables.", "Grandma is barley tea. Grandpa is bicycles. Dad is hair and kimbap and codes.", "Seoyun counted them off on her fingers.", "I have lots of hands. More than Jiho.", "That day's picture diary had six hands drawn in it. The title was “The Hands in Our House.”", "Beneath it, in wobbly letters: “I don't have Mom's hands but I have six hands. So I'm not cold.”", "The autumn parent participation class was “Making Songpyeon” ahead of Chuseok.", "This time Seoyun put the notice on my desk herself. Uncrumpled, with a sticky note. “Dad, come.”", "That weekend I went to my parents' house to learn songpyeon.", "You, making songpyeon? First time in forty years.", "I can't be the worst among the moms. It'd embarrass Seoyun.", "You're an embarrassment from the dough on. Come here.", "The dough was too wet, then too dry. Mother made me knead it three times over. Rice flour settled white on my wrist brace.", "Toward evening, Mother brought out an apron from deep in the wardrobe. A faded floral print. The one she used to wear when I was little.", "Wear this. They said to bring Mom's apron, didn't they?", "This one really is a mom's. Your mom's.", "That made me laugh. And my throat tightened a little.", "They say if you shape songpyeon prettily, you'll have a pretty daughter.", "He already has one.", "…Then he can shape them ugly.", "October. When I opened the classroom door, it went quiet again. Among the mothers, a thirty-seven-year-old man in a faded floral apron.", "But this time, Seoyun waved first. From the last counter by the window, big.", "Dad! Over here!", "The boy from spring, Jihun, asked again. “Her dad came again. Where's your mom?”", "This time Seoyun didn't freeze. Kneading her dough, she answered as if it was nothing.", "We don't have a mom at our house. Instead, Dad learned songpyeon. From Grandma.", "…Guys make songpyeon?", "Yeah. My dad makes kimbap too. He braids hair too. What about your dad?", "Jihun couldn't answer. Beside him, his mom turned away, holding back a laugh.", "My songpyeon looked less like half-moons and more like dumplings. Sesame spilled out the sides. The mothers from nearby counters came over one by one to help.", "Jiho's mom held up one of my songpyeon and laughed out loud.", "Mr. Han, this isn't songpyeon, it's a burst kimbap.", "That's our house style!", "No matter how I look at it, my songpyeon looks like a dumpling.", "(Shape one more ugly songpyeon and put it on Seoyun's plate)", "Seoyun picked it up, studied it for a long time, then placed it right in the middle of the plate where everyone could see.", "This is the representative songpyeon. Because it's the ugliest.", "(Ask the mothers how to shape them properly)", "The mothers took turns teaching me. Seoyun giggled beside me, watching me get scolded by the moms.", "Dad, you get scolded by Grandma and here too.", "Near the end of class, the teacher asked the kids how they felt about today. Seoyun raised her hand.", "In spring I was alone, but today I did it with my dad.", "Sometimes it's inconvenient not having a mom. Like when they tell you to bring an apron.", "But I don't lie anymore, so it's easier. And Dad's apron is Grandma's, so it's a real mom's.", "The classroom fell silent. Then someone clapped. Jiho's mom. The applause spread. The homeroom teacher wiped her eyes.", "Still in the floral apron, I clapped with rice-floured hands. My ears burned.", "At home, Seoyun put the ugliest songpyeon in the freezer. With a name tag, so no one would eat it.", "“Dad's Songpyeon No. 1. Keep.”", "That songpyeon stayed in the freezer for two years until Mother steamed and ate it without knowing. Seoyun didn't speak to her grandma for three days.", "In the autumn of fifth grade, Seoyun brought a friend home for the first time. A girl she'd met at art school.", "In the entryway the girl looked around our home and asked, with a child's honesty:", "Don't you have a mom at your house?", "I froze in the kitchen. My hand stopped mid-flip on the rolled omelet.", "Nope. Instead, my dad's a double serving.", "What's a double serving?", "He makes kimbap, ties my hair, comes in last at running, and makes codes. Does your dad do all that?", "…My dad comes in first at running.", "My dad comes in last but runs the longest. That's harder.", "The girl thought for a moment, then nodded. She looked convinced.", "That day I didn't burn the omelet. Even though my hands were shaking.", "That evening, after her friend went home, Seoyun came and stood beside me as I washed the dishes.", "Dad, you heard earlier, right?", "Heard what?", "Your ears are red. You heard.", "…You said it well.", "I practiced. What to say if someone asked. Since first grade.", "My hand stopped rinsing the plate. Since first grade. That tiny kid had practiced alone for four years.", "She's been practicing alone since first grade.", "(Just hug her with wet hands)", "Ah, Dad! My clothes are getting wet!", "Still, Seoyun didn't pull away. Water dripped from my rubber gloves.", "…Dad, I don't think I need to practice anymore. I did it once today and it was easy.", "“You worked hard. Practicing all alone.”", "It wasn't hard. You practiced with LAN cables too. Our house is a practicing house.", "Instead of answering, I tried to pat her head with my gloved hand, and got scolded for dripping.", "That night I stared for a long time at the family newspaper photo on my phone.", "Five years of running to fill the empty space where her mom had been. But my child hadn't left that space empty. She had given it a different name.", "A double serving. Was I living up to that name? I wasn't sure. All I knew was that tomorrow, I'd still be making kimbap.", "Chapter 4  The Summer She Was Twelve", "Summer vacation of sixth grade. Seoyun's last summer as an elementary schooler.", "Seoyun set that summer's goals herself. In red at the top of her vacation plan.", "“Ride a bike. Go to the sea. Ask Dad about his dream.”", "What's the third one?", "It's homework. “Interview a parent about their childhood dream.” But I'm asking that at the sea. For the mood.", "The first one was already a problem. I couldn't ride a bike. Thirty-nine years old.", "If you can't ride, who's going to teach me?", "The answer was in my parents' yard. Father's thirty-year-old bicycle, and Father.", "Your dad fell once when he was seven and said he'd never ride again. The stubbornness started then.", "I split my knee open.", "Everyone splits their knees. That's how you learn.", "A country road between rice paddies. Father held the back of the seat, Seoyun clung to the handlebars, and I ran behind.", "Grandpa, don't let go! Never let go!", "I won't.", "She fell ten times. Three spots of red antiseptic on her knees. On the eleventh try she bit her lip hard and got back on.", "Don't let go! You can't let go!", "I told you I won't.", "But I saw it. Father's hand leaving the seat. Standing still there with his palm open.", "Seoyun was riding on her own. Wobbly, but not falling. A dragonfly flew along beside her.", "Father's hand has left the seat.", "(Shout) “Seoyun, you're riding by yourself!”", "Seoyun looked back. Seeing no one behind the seat, she screamed and crashed into the paddy ridge.", "Why'd you tell her? They ride best when they don't know.", "Seoyun jumped up from the grass and shouted. Her face wasn't angry.", "I rode! I rode by myself! Grandpa, you liar!", "(Keep quiet and run along with her)", "I didn't say anything. I just ran after her until I was out of breath.", "At the end of the road, Seoyun stopped on her own. Only then did she look back and see her grandpa standing far away.", "…Since when did you let go?", "A while ago.", "I rode! I rode by myself! Grandpa, you liar!", "Seoyun ran to her grandpa and clung to him. Father stood there stiffly, but for a long time he couldn't close his open palm.", "That evening, eating watermelon on the porch, Father spoke so only I could hear.", "Teaching isn't holding on. It's knowing when to let go.", "You'll have to let go someday too. If you keep holding on for fear she'll fall, she'll ride her whole life looking back.", "I'm not ready yet.", "No parent is ever ready. The hand lets go first.", "I only understood those words fully eight years later, at the airport.", "That night, after Seoyun fell asleep, I secretly got on the bicycle in the yard. At thirty-nine.", "I fell three times. On the fourth, the pedals turned. I circled the yard once and looked up to see Seoyun sitting on the porch with her sketchbook.", "I saw everything. I drew it. It's called “Dad Rode Too.”", "…Since when were you watching?", "A while ago.", "August. Our first trip with just the two of us. My first vacation in five years, too.", "I prepared like a programmer. An itinerary in a spreadsheet, a minute-by-minute schedule, emergency contacts, backup plans B and C.", "Dad, why are you planning a trip like a launch schedule?", "Reduce the variables and nothing goes wrong.", "The variables blew up on day one. The moment we reached the East Sea it poured. Plan B was an indoor aquarium, closed that day, and Plan C didn't exist. I'd only written that it did.", "Under the eaves of the guesthouse, we watched the sea in the rain. My spreadsheet was getting soaked in my pocket.", "Sorry. Dad should've checked the weather…", "Dad, look. The sea's a different color when it rains.", "Seoyun took out her sketchbook. Gray, navy, the nameless colors in between. She drew for an hour.", "Postcards are full of the sea on sunny days. This is one only we saw.", "The soaked itinerary is in my pocket.", "(Crumple up the soaked itinerary)", "I took the spreadsheet out of my pocket and threw it in the trash. Strangely, my shoulders felt lighter.", "Oh, Dad threw away his plan. I have to draw this.", "“Tomorrow will definitely be sunny. I'll make a Plan D.”", "Dad. No plans. Let's just stay. Here.", "Seoyun took the pen out of my hand. For the first time I spent an afternoon without a plan. It wasn't as scary as I thought.", "The next day cleared up like magic. At sunset, it was just the two of us on the sand left by the low tide.", "I rolled up my pants and put my feet in the water. Seoyun picked up a stick and started drawing something in the wet sand.", "When I looked back a while later, a hand as big as a house was drawn on the sand. There was a band on the wrist.", "Dad's hand again?", "Yeah. But this time I drew it the biggest. So you can see it from a plane.", "The waves began erasing it from the fingertips. The pinky, the ring finger. Seoyun watched quietly.", "What a shame. It's all getting erased.", "It's fine. You took a picture.", "I had. The moment the drawing was done, out of habit. That photo is in the folder as “v098_sandhand_eastsea.”", "You always take pictures, so I can draw on things that get erased. I like that.", "I thought about that for a long time. Because I was holding things up from behind, she could go forward without fear.", "When the sun touched the horizon, Seoyun held out the stick like a microphone.", "All right, let's start the interview. Mr. Han Jeongwoo, what was your childhood dream?", "(A dream. I had one. Shoved to the bottom of a drawer a long time ago.)", "My childhood dream. Should I tell her the truth?", "“To make my own game. Not a company game, one I wanted to make.”", "What kind of game?", "Not a fighting game… just a story about someone staying beside someone. They said that kind doesn't sell, so I gave up.", "“Hmm. I don't really remember.”", "Liar. Your ears went red. It's homework, so you have to answer for real.", "…To make my own game. Not fighting. A story about someone staying beside someone. They said that kind doesn't sell, so I gave up.", "There was something I'd been making alone in college. A little every weekend. That folder was last modified the year Seoyun was born.", "When did you give up?", "Um… around when you were born.", "I regretted it the moment I said it. The playfulness drained from Seoyun's face.", "Because of me?", "“Because of me?”", "“No. Thanks to you, I got to make something better.”", "What did you make?", "You. And burst kimbap.", "…That's not a game.", "To Dad, it's the hardest, most fun game there is. And you can't even save.", "“…Because I ran out of time. That's the truth.”", "I'd decided not to lie. Ever since she scolded me at seven.", "…Sorry.", "There's nothing to be sorry about. Dad has never once regretted spending that time on you. That's the truth too.", "Seoyun sat on the sand hugging her knees. The waves had reached the palm of the drawn hand.", "Dad, then let's do this.", "Later I'll draw the pictures. You make a game out of them. A story about someone staying beside someone.", "You draw, and Dad makes it?", "Yeah. Then your dream didn't get put away. It just waited until I grew up.", "A twelve-year-old's promise. I laughed and hooked pinkies with her. I didn't think we'd be able to keep it.", "Neither of us knew that ten years later, that promise would land on my desk as a contract for a book that sold three hundred thousand copies.", "Interview over. The homework title is “Dad's Dream Is on Standby.”", "On the express bus home, Seoyun fell asleep leaning on my shoulder. She held a seashell in her hand.", "That seashell is on my monitor stand now. Under the drawing of the hands.", "In autumn, while we were spring-cleaning, Seoyun took a box down from the top of the wardrobe.", "The box I'd put up there and pretended to forget. Wedding photos, first-birthday photos. Photos with three people in them.", "Seoyun sat on the living room floor flipping through them one by one. I froze with the rag in my hand.", "Dad, is this Mom?", "…Yeah.", "I look like Mom. My eyes.", "She was right. Seoyun had her eyes. For five years I'd pretended not to see it.", "Why did you hide these? Because it hurts to look at them?", "She asks why I hid them.", "“Yeah. And I was afraid it'd hurt you to see them.”", "It doesn't hurt. I was curious. Where half my face came from.", "I'd mistaken my own hurt for my child's. I hadn't hidden them for Seoyun. I'd hidden them for me.", "“I didn't hide them. I put them away.”", "On top of the wardrobe? Where you can't reach even with a chair?", "I had nothing to say. At twelve, excuses like that didn't work anymore.", "…You're right, I hid them. Because it was hard for me to look.", "Can I have just one? To keep in my room.", "Seoyun picked a first-birthday photo. A baby in hanbok holding a pencil, two young people laughing on either side.", "Sure. It's your photo.", "I'm not taking it because I'm mad at you. Or because I miss Mom.", "I just… want to know I had two pairs of hands once. And after that you became a double serving.", "Seoyun carried the photo toward her room, then looked back from the doorway.", "And Dad. You look so young in this photo. You had lots of hair.", "That's… something you didn't need to see.", "The door closed. I didn't know then that starting next year, that door would close much more often, and much harder.", "February. Elementary school graduation.", "At the back of the auditorium, parents stood with bouquets. Moms and dads, in pairs. I held two bouquets. A double serving.", "Seoyun received awards for six years of perfect attendance and for art. Coming down from the stage, she saw me, saw the two bouquets, and mouthed: “Dummy.”", "After the ceremony, her homeroom teacher came over. The teacher from the third-grade sports day, her sixth-grade homeroom teacher again.", "Mr. Han, you've worked so hard these six years. You're the only parent who never once missed signing the notice book.", "I signed, but I forgot the supplies a lot.", "You always sent plenty of yogurt bottles. The other kids borrowed them from Seoyun.", "A graduation spread was laid out on the porch at my parents' house. Mother's japchae, the fried chicken Father bought, and the kimbap I made.", "Seoyun stood up and cleared her throat. She was holding a sheet of drawing paper.", "I will also present an award. The recipient: Han Jeongwoo.", "On the paper was a certificate with a crayon border. Yellow colored pencil instead of gold foil.", "“Double Serving Award. The above person did Mom's share and Dad's share alone for six years and never gave up even when the kimbap burst; therefore this award is presented. Elementary school graduate Han Seoyun.”", "An award from a twelve-year-old.", "(Accept the certificate with both hands) “Thank you. I'll keep bursting them with all my heart.”", "That was my entire acceptance speech. If I said more, I'd cry.", "Even accepting an award, his ears turn red.", "Well received. He deserves it.", "“But Dad didn't do anything…”", "Dad. If the person giving the award says you get it, why are you re-judging it?", "That's a sickness, I tell you. When someone gives you something, take it.", "Sheepishly, I accepted the certificate. Receiving had always been harder than giving.", "I framed the certificate and set it on my desk at work. The drawing of the hands, the seashell, the Double Serving Award. The area around my monitor was slowly becoming a museum.", "Before March, we went to get her school uniform fitted. Seoyun came out of the fitting room looking like someone I'd never met. The skirt reached her knees; her shoulders had grown broad.", "It suits you. Just one picture…", "Ugh, don't. It's embarrassing.", "I'd never heard that before. Seoyun dodging my camera.", "While I paid, Seoyun waited outside the shop. Three steps away from me.", "On the way home those three steps didn't shrink. Suddenly I thought of Father standing on the paddy road with his palm open.", "(He said it's knowing when to let go. …Is this it? Already?)", "That spring, a hand-drawn sign went up on Seoyun's door.", "Chapter 5  The Closed Door", "Once she started middle school, Seoyun began closing her door.", "A hand-drawn sign went up on it. A drawing of a knocking hand, and beneath it, small: “Please.”", "One semester was enough for the voice that used to call “Dad” to turn into “Yeah.” and “Never mind.”", "She stopped coming to work with me on Saturdays. The floor beside my chair felt empty.", "Hey, isn't Seoyun coming anymore? The design team girls keep asking.", "Minho Park, the new hire that year, had seen Seoyun's last Saturday exactly once.", "Puberty. These days I see her door more often than her face.", "Aw, I didn't talk to my dad for three years at that age either. But I never missed a meal.", "Is that supposed to be comforting?", "As long as she's eating, it's fine.", "One night, a small sound came from behind the closed door. It sounded almost like crying.", "Seoyun. Is something wrong?", "…Dad. Don't come in.", "My… stomach hurts. And… I'm bleeding.", "My mind went blank. And then, I understood what this was.", "No book, no job, no one had ever taught me about this moment.", "My child, behind the door. What do I do?", "(Run to the convenience store)", "The convenience store at midnight. Standing at the shelf, I was more serious than when reading a bug report.", "With wings, regular, medium, large. I didn't know the difference, so I grabbed one of every kind.", "The woman at the counter looked at the basket, looked at me once, and silently added a hand warmer.", "For your daughter? Keep her tummy warm. This one's on me.", "(Call Mother)", "What is it at this hour? …Oh my. Well, it's time.", "Go to the convenience store right now. You don't need to know the kinds, just buy them all. And a hand warmer.", "And Jeongwoo. Don't make a fuss. It's something to celebrate.", "The convenience store at midnight. I grabbed one of every kind, just as Mother told me.", "I set the bag down in front of her door and knocked twice.", "I left it outside. I bought every kind, so use whichever fits. There's a hand warmer too.", "And Seoyun. They say this isn't being sick, it's growing up. Congratulations.", "After a long while, the door opened a hand's width. A hand came out and pulled the bag inside.", "…Dad.", "Yeah.", "Large is way too big.", "The door closed again. Behind it, very softly, came a laugh.", "I sat down in the hallway and stayed there a long time. For the first time, the words “double serving” felt heavy.", "From that day on, I started leaving sliced fruit outside her door.", "Apples, pears, sometimes the peaches Seoyun loved. Set the plate down, knock, turn away.", "In the morning the plate was always empty and set back outside the door. That was our conversation.", "In the spring of eighth grade, the school called while I was at work.", "Mr. Han, Seoyun had a fight with a classmate. Could you come in now?", "I bolted out of the meeting. Company ID, wrist brace, hair unwashed for three days, all as it was.", "In the teachers' office, Seoyun stood with a split lip. The other kid had traces of a nosebleed.", "They say the other student said something cruel first. “The kid with no mom.”", "My blood boiled. But the moment Seoyun saw me, she turned her head away.", "Why'd you come? It's embarrassing.", "That hurt more than a fist. Her eyes swept over my company ID and my rumpled hoodie.", "“It's embarrassing.”", "“I came even if it's embarrassing. You need at least one person on your side.”", "Her shoulders trembled, just slightly. Still, she never looked at me.", "(Say nothing)", "I opened my mouth, then closed it. The other parent was in a suit.", "(Right. She has reason to be embarrassed. With a dad like this.)", "In the car home, Seoyun only looked out the window. The moment we got home, she exploded.", "You don't know anything!", "Do you know what the kids say? At lunch, changing for gym, whenever anyone mentions moms, they all look at me!", "If I had a mom, I could ask her all this stuff. I can't ask you!", "You can ask me. I'll look everything up.", "Not things you look up! I need someone who just knows!", "The door slammed. Hard enough to shake the whole apartment.", "I stood there for a long time. Not one word she'd said was wrong.", "That night I sliced fruit again. My hands shook so much the apple peel kept breaking.", "As I set the plate down, I stuck a sticky note on it. “Did you eat?”", "Morning. The plate was empty, and on the back of the sticky note was a drawing.", "A drawing of an empty plate. And wobbly letters: “Ate. Sorry about yesterday.”", "I put that sticky note in my wallet. It's still there.", "Closing the door is part of growing up. There's a part she has to grow alone, inside that door.", "It's because I can't do a mom's share for her.", "You do your share. Plenty of kids with moms in this world don't get a mom's share either.", "A parent's job is to keep watch outside the door. Opening it is the child's job.", "Father had never been the kind of man to say things like that. I looked at him, a little surprised.", "What. I stood outside your door too. You won't remember.", "Father says he stood outside a door too.", "“You too, Father? …I didn't know.”", "You're not supposed to know. From inside the door, you can't see outside it.", "That day, for the first time, standing outside a closed door felt a little less lonely.", "“I can't do it as well as you did.”", "I didn't do it well either. I just stood there.", "Standing there is the hardest part. Most people either break the door down or walk away.", "Autumn of ninth grade. There was an art exhibition at Seoyun's school festival.", "Seoyun just left the notice on the table and said nothing. I knew that silence was an invitation.", "And of all days, the server went down that day.", "The outage recovery finished at nine p.m. The festival ended at six.", "The face reflected in the convenience store window outside our building was the face of a man who'd broken a promise.", "The festival ended three hours ago.", "(Go to the school anyway)", "The school gate was closed. The security guard came out with a flashlight.", "Han Seoyun's dad? Your daughter left this with me. Said you'd come, even if late.", "It was the exhibition pamphlet. Seoyun's work was circled. <The Plate Outside the Door>.", "(Buy a cake and go home)", "I stood in the entryway holding the cake box. I didn't know what to say.", "The exhibition pamphlet was on the table. Seoyun's work was circled. <The Plate Outside the Door>.", "That night I stood outside her closed door. Empty-handed instead of with a fruit plate.", "Seoyun. Dad didn't make it. I'm sorry. I won't make excuses.", "It was quiet for a long time. Then the door opened. For the first time in two years, all the way.", "I know. The server crashed. Uncle Minho texted me.", "There've been days you couldn't come, but never a day you didn't.", "Seoyun brought a framed picture from her desk. In it were a dark hallway, a closed door, and a plate of apples set in front of it.", "And a big hand setting down the plate. A black brace on the wrist.", "I won a prize. Silver. Gold went to a landscape, but mine's drawn better.", "…Even Dad can see that.", "You can't tell good art.", "I can tell this one. It's my hand.", "That Friday it rained. Seoyun hadn't taken an umbrella.", "I took a half day and stood at the school gate. I had only one umbrella. As always. I only ever thought of hers.", "Why do you always bring just one umbrella? What about you?", "Dad's fine getting a little wet.", "Are you dumb?", "Seoyun tilted the umbrella toward me. Somehow she'd grown up to my shoulder.", "We walked home, both half soaked. That day Seoyun didn't close her door.", "Chapter 6  A Hundred Backs", "In high school, Seoyun announced she was taking the art college entrance exams.", "I'm going to make a living with drawing. Opposing me won't help.", "I'm not opposing you.", "…Oppose me a little. You're taking the wind out of my sails.", "The fees for an exam-prep art academy were not a number I knew. A third of my salary went straight out.", "That night I posted a profile on a freelancing site. “Server and client. Weekend work. Rush jobs welcome.”", "Weekdays at the office, weekends freelancing. The wrist brace spread to both hands.", "Hey, what do you do on weekends these days? The bags under your eyes are in dark mode.", "A hobby.", "Your hobby is overtime?", "I didn't tell Seoyun. I didn't want her thinking of tuition every time she picked up a pencil.", "Every night Seoyun came home from the academy at eleven. Her fingertips were always black with charcoal.", "Dad, give me your hand.", "Why?", "Homework. A hundred hand croquis.", "I rested my hand on the table and sat for an hour. Her eyes traveled over the tendons on the back of my hand, the stiff knuckles, the marks from the brace.", "Your hand's gotten old.", "Thanks.", "It's a compliment. It means there's a lot to draw.", "In the summer of eleventh grade, an unknown number called. A voice I hadn't heard in ten years.", "My ex-wife. She said she'd remarried and was doing well. And that she wanted to see Seoyun once.", "(Ten years. A woman who wasn't there for the entrance ceremony, the sports day, or the convenience store that night.)", "A voice I haven't heard in ten years.", "“I'll ask Seoyun. It's her decision.”", "After hanging up, I spent a long time catching my breath. It wasn't that I wasn't angry. But that anger was mine to carry, not Seoyun's.", "“What right do you have, after all this time?”", "The other end went quiet. I hung up and spent a long time catching my breath.", "(…No. This isn't mine to decide. She's Seoyun's mother too.)", "That night I told Seoyun. She stopped sharpening her pencil and sat still for a long time.", "Do you want me to meet her?", "What I want doesn't matter. If you're curious, meet her; if not, don't. Either way, Dad's here.", "…I'll see her once. I was kind of curious. I don't remember what she looks like.", "On the day, I waited on the street across from the café. Through the window I could see the two of them sitting face to face.", "An hour later Seoyun came out. I couldn't read her expression.", "She was pretty. And she's doing well. Without me.", "…", "She said she was sorry. But she didn't ask a single thing about you. What you live on, whether your wrist is okay.", "So I realized. She's a guest. A guest who visits sometimes.", "Seoyun took my arm. Just like on her first day of elementary school, right over the wrist brace.", "Dad, did you eat?", "Not yet.", "Me neither. Let's have kimbap. Store-bought, not yours.", "That day Seoyun didn't bring up her mother again. And after that, not even once.", "Spring of twelfth grade. The national art competition.", "Seoyun prepared for three months. She didn't even get an honorable mention.", "That night she didn't come home from the academy. After midnight, I went there.", "In the dark studio, a single desk lamp was on. Seoyun was curled up in front of an easel.", "I guess I have no talent. After ten years of drawing.", "Such a waste of tuition. And of your weekends.", "…Weekends?", "You think I'm stupid? I've seen you asleep at the kitchen table at four a.m. so many times.", "Caught. Since when? I was at a loss for words.", "She found out about my weekend freelancing.", "“It's not a waste. Those are the best-spent weekends of my life.”", "Liar. Your ears didn't go red. …So it's true.", "“You caught me. Sorry I hid it.”", "Don't say sorry. It makes me feel like the bad kid.", "I took out my phone and opened a folder. Its name was “seoyun_art.”", "Inside were dated folders, ten years' worth. Every scanned drawing had a file name.", "“v001_dadhand_crayon.” “v047_lastplacedad_silver.” “v212_plate_outside_door.”", "What is this…", "Version control. Dad backs up everything important.", "Look from the start. v001 and now. Is this ten years of someone with no talent?", "Seoyun swiped through the screen. From a seven-year-old's wobbly hand to an eighteen-year-old's hand with every tendon alive.", "Partway through, she started crying. Silently, rubbing her eyes with the charcoal-stained back of her hand.", "Why are the file names so lame?", "Because I'm a programmer.", "There are commit messages too? …“Drew a rabbit today. Looks like a pig but the artist insists it's a rabbit.” You drew that one!", "Seoyun laughed through her tears. Then she pulled a sketchbook out from behind the easel.", "I hid something too.", "The sketchbook was full of my back. My back asleep at the table, in front of the monitor, setting down a plate in the hallway.", "On the last page was a number. 100.", "The hundred hand croquis were homework. These I just drew. A hundred of your back.", "I failed the competition, but no matter what anyone says, I draw this better than anyone. In the world.", "Under the desk lamp, we spent a long time flipping through each other's ten years.", "That summer, the academy director called me in separately.", "He said Seoyun's drawings didn't fit the mold of Korean exam art, but were a perfect fit for Japanese universities in character design and manga. There was a good school in Kyoto.", "Seoyun had taken the brochure but never showed it to me. I found it under the recycling bag.", "(Tuition, dorm fees, plane tickets. …And me, left alone in this apartment.)", "Will you send her?", "I have to. That's where her drawing needs to go, they say.", "And money?", "I'll take on more freelancing.", "You don't have only two wrists in this world.", "Father went into his room and came back with a bankbook.", "I've been putting money in since the day Seoyun was born. It's not much. I thought your mother didn't know.", "I knew. Because I put money in too.", "The bankbook my parents are holding out.", "“…I'll accept it. Thank you.”", "You finally know how to lean.", "I remembered being told, on this porch ten years earlier, to learn how to lean. This time I bowed my head and took it.", "“This is your retirement money. I can't take it.”", "Our retirement is watching Seoyun draw. Take it.", "You have to know how to receive to know how to give.", "After a long while, I bowed my head and took it.", "That night I ironed the crumpled brochure and put it on Seoyun's desk. With a sticky note.", "“Apply. Worrying about money is the grown-ups' job. — Dad (a double serving), Grandpa, Grandma”", "Chapter 7  The Last Winter", "Once the applications were in, our fridge turned into a Japanese classroom.", "Seoyun stuck hiragana cards on the fridge door. To get the milk out, you had to read “あ.”", "You memorize them too, Dad. You have to play the interviewer when I practice.", "Dad's forty-six. The last new language I learned was Python.", "Python's a snake. These are letters. Easier.", "And so at forty-six I learned hiragana. With cards taped inside a stall in the office restroom.", "Boss, there's a “ぬ” taped up in the restroom. That's yours, isn't it?", "It's “nu.” I keep mixing it up with “me,” so I put it up.", "I thought it was a drawing of an earthworm.", "Every night we practiced interviews at the kitchen table. I played the professor. Seoyun wrote the questions, and I read them without understanding a word.", "Anata wa… naze… kono daigaku o… erabimashita ka.", "Your pronunciation makes me less nervous. Good. Once more.", "Seoyun answered at length in Japanese. I didn't understand a single word. But in the middle I heard “otōsan” three times.", "You just talked about Dad, didn't you?", "…No.", "Otōsan means dad. I memorized that one.", "Ugh, I shouldn't have taught you.", "I heard “otōsan” three times.", "“Can't you tell me what you said?”", "No. Interview answers are trade secrets.", "I'll tell you if I get in. If I fail, it stays secret forever.", "(Pretend not to notice and read the next question)", "I decided not to ask. Some words are meant to be heard when the speaker is ready to say them. Ten years outside a door had taught me that.", "…You're not asking.", "You'll have your own timing.", "Yeah. I do.", "December. The portfolio deadline approached. She had to choose twenty pieces.", "Seoyun had spread about a hundred drawings across the academy studio floor and sat in the middle of them.", "I can't choose. They all seem bad, and I can't let any of them go.", "I opened the “seoyun_art” folder on my phone. It was past v300 now.", "It'd be cheating if Dad chose, right?", "You can't tell good art.", "I can't tell art, but I can read logs. I know which ones you kept working on the longest.", "I sorted by file modification time. The drawings Seoyun had reworked for days rose to the top.", "A man running dead last. The plate outside the door. A back asleep at the table. Grandpa fixing a bicycle. A wrinkled hand pouring barley tea.", "…They're all people. And all people from our house.", "You finished the landscapes in a day. The people you held on to for a week each.", "Seoyun looked at the screen for a long time. Then she picked those drawings up off the floor one by one.", "My teacher says to put in a variety. Still life, landscape, figures, evenly.", "Twenty pieces. What should go in?", "“Put in the ones you looked at the longest. Those are your drawings.”", "And if I fail?", "Then that school doesn't know how to see people.", "…Dad, that was kind of cool just now. If only your ears weren't red.", "“Wouldn't it be safer to mix them evenly, like your teacher says?”", "Safe is boring. You didn't live a safe life either.", "Dad wanted to live safely.", "But you had me. That's the least safe thing there is.", "All twenty became people. On the cover, she put the hand drawing from age seven beside the hand drawing from age nineteen.", "Seoyun gave it a title. <The Same Hand, Twelve Years>.", "That winter, there was one more incident.", "Ten at night, I heard someone at the front door. Through the peephole I saw Seoyun standing with someone. A boy in a school uniform.", "The boy handed Seoyun something, bowed his head, and ran down the stairs.", "Seoyun came in holding a wrapped scarf. Her cheeks were red. Not just from the cold, it seemed.", "(It's come. Here it comes again. This is harder than kimbap.)", "A scarf seen through the peephole.", "(Pretend I saw nothing and watch TV)", "I was watching the switched-off TV very seriously.", "Dad, that's off.", "…It's in power-saving mode.", "You saw everything. Through the peephole.", "“Who is he. Name, grade, class, career goals.”", "Dad, are you an interviewer?", "These days Dad conducts interviews in Japanese.", "A friend from the academy. Just a friend. …Until he confessed just now.", "Seoyun plopped down on the sofa. She set the scarf on her lap and stared down at it for a long time.", "I turned him down.", "Oh… okay. Why?", "I'm going to Japan in three months. And… he's nice to me, but he doesn't look at my drawings. He just says they're pretty.", "I like someone who looks at my drawings for a long time. Like you, even if they can't tell good art, someone who looks for a long time.", "My chest ached strangely. Being my daughter's standard was both an honor and a responsibility.", "Seoyun. Someone like Dad might be a little boring.", "I know. They don't make your heart flutter.", "Neither of us knew what those words would come to mean for me later.", "But flutters pass quickly. Looking for a long time doesn't pass. I learned that from you.", "Seoyun said she politely returned the scarf the next day. And that she drew the boy a picture.", "When I asked what she drew, Seoyun laughed. “A drawing of the scarf. We'll call it received.”", "At the end of December we made kimjang at my parents' house. It was the last kimjang the four of us would make together.", "For the first time, Mother taught Seoyun the seasoning ratios. Moving forty years of eyeballing into measuring cups.", "Write it down. When I die, this taste is gone.", "Grandma, don't say that.", "I'm not saying I'm dying. I'm saying write it down. Your dad backs up everything, doesn't he?", "Seoyun took notes on her phone, then pulled out her sketchbook. She began drawing Mother's hands, dusted with red pepper flakes.", "Father, carrying cabbages in the yard, stood behind her for a long time.", "Aren't you going to draw my hands?", "I drew yours last time, Grandpa. Fixing the bicycle.", "Draw them once more. So you can look at them in Japan.", "Seoyun turned the page, laughing. Father rested his dirt-covered hands on his knees and, for the first time in his life, sat as a model. Not moving an inch.", "Seoyun is drawing Grandpa's hands.", "(Put my hand beside theirs)", "I can draw your hand with my eyes closed now, Dad.", "Draw it once more anyway. So you can look at it in Japan.", "Seoyun drew three people's hands on one page. A hand with pepper flakes, a hand with dirt, a hand in a brace.", "(Take a photo of the scene)", "A granddaughter drawing and a grandfather modeling. I took a photo and put it in the folder. File name: “v319_father_firstmodel.”", "Dad, backing up again?", "This one I have to.", "While the pork boiled, Father spoke abruptly.", "Seoyun. If it's hard over there, just come home. It's nothing to be ashamed of.", "I won't come back.", "You don't have to. I'm saying go knowing there's a place to come back to.", "Seoyun nodded, then suddenly buried her face in her grandfather's back. Father froze, flustered, his dirt-covered hands raised in the air.", "January. The interview. Seoyun went to Kyoto on her own. Her first time on a plane.", "I only took her as far as the airport, then spent three days at the office staring at my phone. Minho did my code reviews for me.", "When she got back, Seoyun chattered in the entryway without even taking off her shoes. The Kyoto river, the tatami in the dorm, the mystery fish in the in-flight meal.", "So. The interview.", "The professor looked through my portfolio for a long time. All twenty pages, without skipping one.", "Did he ask why you chose that school?", "Yeah. I answered. Otōsan came up four times.", "Not three?", "It went up in the real thing. …The content is still a secret. I said I'd tell you if I got in.", "Only then did she start taking off her shoes, then looked back to add one thing.", "The professor smiled. And he said, “Show me that character when it's finished.”", "Character? What character?", "I told you, it's a secret.", "I would have to wait two more years to hear that secret.", "Chapter 8  Kansai, 7:40 a.m.", "Two a.m., one night in February. The light in Seoyun's room was still on. The door was open. It was always open now.", "Still up? I made you cocoa.", "Seoyun sat frozen in front of her monitor. The screen was full of Japanese.", "Dad, I… got in. The art university in Kyoto. Character design.", "I almost dropped the mug. Cocoa splashed onto the back of my hand.", "You got in? Really? Read it again. Are you sure you didn't misread the Japanese?", "I read it three times. “Accepted” is in kanji, I can't get it wrong.", "I tried to lift Seoyun up in a hug, heard something in my back, and stopped. Instead I messed up her hair like crazy.", "Ah, my hair! …But Dad.", "Maybe I shouldn't go. Grandpa's bankbook, that's their retirement. And leaving you here alone, too.", "Who's going to ask whether you've eaten?", "“Who's going to ask whether you've eaten?”", "“Go. Don't worry about Dad.”", "…You're always like that. Never saying anything about yourself.", "Fine. I'll go. But later you have to listen to everything I have to say.", "“Honestly, Dad's scared too. But go.”", "That's the first time I've heard you say you're scared.", "I'm scared. Of this place going quiet, and of not being able to get to you when you're sick.", "…Then I have to go even more. If you're putting up with being scared, I can't not go.", "Then tell me your interview answer now. You said you'd tell me if you got in.", "…When you come to Kyoto. You have to hear that there.", "Dad, there's something I'm going to draw there. Something I've wanted to draw for a long time.", "What is it?", "Still a secret. When it's done, I'll show you first… no, I'll show you last.", "What kind of order is that?", "My order.", "Three months until departure. We made a to-do list. True to being a programmer's daughter, Seoyun drew it as a spreadsheet.", "Passport, visa, dorm application, currency exchange. And in the bottom row, a line Seoyun wrote in herself.", "“Learn to roll kimbap.”", "So I can make it there. Your kimbap.", "My kimbap bursts.", "Exactly. I have to learn how to burst it.", "Sunday afternoon, we laid out seaweed, rice, and pickled radish on the table. The same table as four a.m. thirteen years ago.", "Spread the rice thin. Get greedy and it bursts.", "So you burst yours because you got greedy.", "Dad's burst because I wanted to give you lots of filling. That's not greed, it's…", "Love. I know.", "The first kimbap Seoyun rolled burst at the side, as if on cue. She held it up and laughed for a long time.", "It's hereditary.", "Burst kimbap, hereditary, she says.", "“I passed that down too. Sorry.”", "Why sorry? I like it. It's a sign it's ours.", "“Burst or not, it tastes the same. That's our family motto.”", "Our family motto is kimbap? I love it. I'll write it up on my wall in Kyoto.", "We shared the burst kimbap. It really did taste the same.", "A week before departure, at my parents' house.", "Mother packed a whole box of gochujang, seaweed, and barley tea bags. Father silently oiled the wheels of Seoyun's suitcase.", "They say the water in Japan tastes different. Boil this barley tea. It'll taste like home.", "Grandma, don't say things like that, I'm going to cry.", "Cry. Cry it all out here and smile at the airport. Your dad will be the one crying there.", "I won't cry.", "You will. Your ears will go red first.", "Seoyun lay on the porch and drew one last picture. The same spot, the same pose, as thirteen years ago.", "This time there were four hands. Two wrinkled hands, one callused hand, and on top of them, one charcoal-stained hand.", "Put this up here. Until I come back.", "The departure hall at Incheon Airport. Her art bag was bigger than her suitcase.", "There was a new hairpin in Seoyun's bangs. The same yellow, which I'd bought last night at the same stationery shop.", "Dad, go on some dates while I'm gone. I mean it.", "Don't worry about Dad. Just make sure you eat properly.", "I'm going to be someone who draws characters. Characters that make people's hearts warm.", "I've already decided my first model. It's a secret, though.", "Character No. 1?", "Seoyun's eyes went wide. She hadn't expected me to remember the sports day sign.", "…You remember.", "Dad backs up everything important.", "The boarding announcement sounded. Seoyun hugged me hard, once. Her hairpin pressed into my shoulder.", "Dad, pack two umbrellas. Not mine anymore. Yours.", "Seoyun went in without looking back once. Because she knew she'd cry if she did.", "I waved until I couldn't see her. My ears burned.", "When I lowered my hand, my palm was open. Like Father on the paddy road eight years before.", "(I let go, Father. She's going just fine, without falling.)", "When I got home, there was a note on the table.", "“Your kimbap was the best in the world. Burst ones included.”", "The apartment we'd shared was too quiet for one. I'd never realized how loud the fridge was.", "Out of habit I started slicing fruit, then stopped. There was no door to set the plate in front of.", "That night, for the first time, I slept with the light on in Seoyun's room.", "Chapter 9  The Empty House", "I had to learn to live alone again. For the first time since my twenties.", "The rice cooker didn't know how to make one serving, and the washing machine was never even half full. Sundays were absurdly long.", "Every Sunday night, photos arrived from Kyoto. The view from the dorm window, the river by the school, pencil shavings.", "And always the same line. “Dad, did you eat?”", "I started to take a photo of a convenience store bento, stopped, and boiled a pot of ramen at least, and sent a photo of that.", "After about a month, Seoyun's texts changed a little.", "(text) Dad, what do you do on weekends these days?", "(reply) Sleep.", "(text) That's it? Meet some people. I meant what I said at the airport.", "I turned my phone face down. Meeting people. At forty-seven. How did that go again?", "Boss, want to go on a blind date? She's a coworker of my cousin.", "Minho. I'm forty-seven.", "She's forty-three. Perfect.", "And I have a daughter.", "Is that a flaw? That's something to brag about.", "A blind date. At forty-seven.", "“…I'll go once.”", "Oh! Finally! I'll pick your outfit. Not that hoodie.", "What's wrong with it?", "You'd show up with your company ID around your neck.", "“Forget it. Who'd want someone like me?”", "Boss. What do you think Seoyun would say if she heard that?", "…I had nothing to say. Seoyun would definitely say, “Are you dumb?”", "…I'll go once.", "The first. The woman Minho introduced.", "I wore the shirt Minho picked and arrived thirty minutes early. I drank three glasses of water.", "I make games for a living. And I have a daughter. She's studying art in Kyoto right now.", "Once I started talking about my daughter, I couldn't stop. Sports day, the hairpin, a hundred backs. When I came to, forty minutes had passed.", "You really love your daughter.", "Oh, I'm sorry. I only talked about myself.", "You seem like a good man. But a grown daughter… it feels a bit heavy for me.", "I wonder if there'd be any room for me in that space.", "She wasn't wrong. I paid for the coffee, said a polite goodbye, and left.", "The second. Someone from the company next door, whom I met at an office board game club.", "We had dinner three times. Each time I visited the restaurant beforehand and memorized the menu.", "From the day I tried to set up a fourth, her replies slowed. A day, three days, a week. Then they stopped.", "I never heard why. A rejection without a reason felt like it was all my fault.", "The third. Someone I met at a client meeting. Her name was Eunjeong. We saw each other for three months.", "The Han River in March. The wind off the water was still cold, but we often walked after work. She liked walking, and I liked what she liked.", "For the first time I talked about Seoyun slowly, at length, and she listened to the end. So I hoped for more.", "I could write a whole book about your daughter by now. So what about you, Jeongwoo?", "Me?", "What you like. Not your daughter, not your company. What you do when you're alone.", "I opened my mouth to answer, and nothing came out. Kimbap, sports day, the backup folder. Everything that came to mind was Seoyun's.", "What do I like?", "“…I don't know. I've never thought about it.”", "Then it's homework. Find just one thing by the next time we meet.", "I never did that homework. Whenever I tried, Seoyun's Sunday photos came to mind first.", "“I like what you like, Eunjeong.”", "…That's not an answer.", "She laughed. But it was a slightly cool laugh, like the wind off the river.", "Early April, her birthday. I'd been taking notes on everything she'd said for three months. Backups were my specialty.", "She'd said she liked red bean buns. The ones from the neighborhood bakery her father used to bring home after work when she was little.", "From the name of a neighborhood she'd mentioned in passing, I found that bakery. Two subway transfers away.", "Anyone who remembers this bun must be a good person. I put in an extra one.", "She took the bag and was silent for a long time. Whether it was a happy face or a troubled one, I couldn't tell.", "Jeongwoo… why do you go this far?", "Because you're someone I like.", "…Thank you. Really.", "She split the bun in half and gave me half. The red bean was sweet. It turned out to be the last thing we ever shared.", "On an evening with rain in the forecast, she asked to meet first.", "You're a really good man, Jeongwoo. You never break a promise, you remember everything I say. Even the red bean buns.", "But… you're too nice. It… doesn't make my heart flutter.", "You're always standing one step back, on my side. In three months I never once heard what you want.", "I'm sorry. I guess I still want to feel that flutter.", "“It doesn't make my heart flutter.”", "“Thank you for being honest.”", "I meant it. For three months she had been kind to me. It was just that I wasn't someone who made hearts flutter.", "That “just” lodged in my chest like a nail.", "“…Would it have been different if I'd done more?”", "No. It's not that you did too little. It's just… how it is.", "Just how it is. A reason that couldn't even be fixed.", "When I left the café, it started to rain. I had no umbrella. Seoyun had told me to pack two, and I hadn't packed even one.", "(So being called nice can hurt this much.)", "(Was it the same with my ex-wife? Did she go to someone else because I didn't make her heart flutter?)", "(Am I someone no one will ever choose?)", "The face reflected in the convenience store glass looked strange and old. Rain ran down my glasses.", "To the man in the glass.", "“…It's my fault. Because I'm not enough.”", "I said that to the man in the glass. He didn't argue.", "“Still, I have to go to work tomorrow.”", "All I could do was keep doing tomorrow what I did today. That was the one thing I was good at.", "I bought a plastic umbrella at the convenience store. Three thousand won. The same price as the hairpin fourteen years ago.", "That night a photo came from Seoyun. Cherry blossoms along the Kamo River. “Dad, did you eat?”", "(I replied that I had. It was a lie.)", "I opened the plastic umbrella and walked to the bus stop.", "Of course, I had no idea that three-thousand-won umbrella would change my life.", "Chapter 10  One Umbrella", "By the time I reached the bus stop, the rain had become a downpour.", "There was no one under the shelter. No, there was one person. Outside it.", "A woman was crouched on the sidewalk. The bottom of a cardboard box had burst, and dozens of books had spilled onto the wet paving stones.", "She had taken off her cardigan and laid it over the books, and was picking them up one by one with bare hands, hugging them to her chest. Rain dripped from her hair.", "(She's going to catch a cold like that.)", "My body moved before I could think. I ran over and tilted my umbrella over her and the books.", "Huh…?", "Pick up the books first. I'll hold this.", "You're getting soaked!", "Books are ruined when they get wet. I just dry off.", "She looked up at me. Her round glasses were covered in raindrops. Behind them, her eyes paused for a moment.", "…You're a strange one.", "I hear that a lot.", "We picked up the books in silence. Holding the umbrella in one hand, I picked up books with the other and stacked them in her arms.", "They were all Japanese books. Thin, pretty books full of pictures.", "The last one lay face down at the edge of a puddle. I picked it up and wiped off the rain with my sleeve.", "The cover was still facing down. I handed it to her without turning it over.", "…If I'd turned it over then, would everything have come untangled a little sooner?", "Thank you. I just picked these up from customs today. I didn't know the box was this flimsy.", "Do you run a bookstore?", "A small one. I love illustrated essays, so I bring in Japanese ones little by little.", "Then a bus came. Her bus. Not mine.", "Her bus has come.", "(Hand her the umbrella) “Take this. Your books will get wet.”", "What? Then what about you?", "I'll be home in no time once I'm on the bus.", "A lie. My bus was twenty minutes away, and after getting off I had a ten-minute walk.", "She took the umbrella, hesitated a long while, then got on the bus.", "I'll definitely give it back! I'm at Comm—", "The doors closed. The rest was drowned out by the rain.", "(Hold the umbrella over her up to the bus door)", "I held the umbrella over her until she got on the bus. Then, just before the doors closed, I folded it and pushed it inside.", "Use it when you get off. I'll be home in no time once I'm on the bus.", "A lie. My bus was twenty minutes away, and after getting off I had a ten-minute walk.", "Excuse me! I'll definitely give this back! I'm at Comm—", "The doors closed. The rest was drowned out by the rain.", "Where the bus had been, a slip of paper lay on the ground. A wet bookmark.", "The stamp on it was half smudged. “Comm…” The rest was unreadable.", "I picked it up and put it in my wallet. I don't know why. Next to Seoyun's sticky note.", "Walking home, I thought: I got dumped today, got drenched, and my three-thousand-won umbrella had more or less become someone else's.", "(So why don't I feel that bad?)", "The next day I caught a cold. I didn't tell Seoyun.", "Three weeks passed. The bookmark dried and crinkled in my wallet.", "I never thought of going to look. I couldn't have found it from smudged letters anyway, and above all, I had no reason to.", "Boss, why do you keep staring out the window on rainy days?", "I wasn't.", "You're doing it right now.", "That Sunday, Seoyun asked me a favor.", "(text) Dad, can you send me some Korean picture books? I need them for an assignment. Pick them yourself. Don't order online.", "(reply) You know I can't tell good art.", "(text) That's why it's fun. I need ones picked with your eyes.", "The big bookstore was too big to know what to choose. I wandered for an hour and came out empty-handed.", "On the way back, I took a wrong turn in an alley behind the office. A neighborhood I'd walked for ten years, and an alley I'd never seen.", "At the end of the alley was a small sign. Handwritten on a wooden board.", "“Comma Books.”", "(Comm… a.)", "I thought of the bookmark in my wallet. No way. How many bookstores are there in Seoul?", "When I pushed the door, a bell rang. The smell of books, and a nutty smell. Barley tea.", "Welcome— Oh?", "She stood up from behind the counter. Round glasses, mustard cardigan. Not soaked, this time.", "Umbrella Guy!", "…Book Lady.", "Having said it, we both laughed, embarrassed.", "I waited three weeks. To give your umbrella back. I went to that bus stop every rainy day, but you never came.", "I don't usually use that stop. I just walked there that one day.", "So it was a one-day coincidence.", "Today's a coincidence too. I took a wrong turn.", "Do you know what you call a coincidence that happens twice?", "A bug?", "…You're a programmer.", "Laughing, she brought a plastic umbrella out from a back shelf. Neatly dried, with a ribbon tied on the handle.", "She holds out the neatly dried umbrella.", "“Keep it. You can lend it to customers on rainy days.”", "Then I'll name this umbrella “Umbrella Guy.” Rental No. 1.", "A three-thousand-won umbrella moving up in the world.", "“Thank you. You even tied a ribbon on it.”", "I took the umbrella. A three-thousand-won plastic umbrella looked like a gift.", "Thanks to you that day, not a single book was damaged. They were worth far more than the umbrella.", "Have a cup of barley tea before you go. We serve this instead of water.", "I took the cup and sipped. For a moment I thought of the porch at my parents' house.", "…It tastes like my mother's barley tea.", "That's the highest compliment.", "I explained Seoyun's request. My daughter in Kyoto, picture books, “picked with Dad's eyes.”", "Your daughter studies art in Kyoto?", "Yes. Character design. A kid who drew nothing but my hands since she was seven.", "Her hand paused on the shelf for a moment. Just a moment.", "…Hands.", "Yes. She drew them much bigger than they really are.", "I see.", "She looked like she was about to say something and swallowed it. But she soon turned and pulled out a few picture books.", "Choose from these yourself. If I choose, it won't be “Dad's eyes.”", "I stood for thirty minutes flipping through them. Then I stopped at one. A picture book about a grandmother brewing barley tea for her grandchild.", "I'll take this one.", "Why did you choose that one?", "I don't know about the art. It just seems to have a smell. This book.", "She looked at me quietly. The same eyes that had looked up at me at the bus stop.", "I see why your daughter wanted to borrow her dad's eyes.", "When I paid and was about to leave, she spoke.", "I'm Yoon Jisoo. Not Book Lady. I'm past forty, a bit old for “lady.”", "Han Jeongwoo. And “guy”… is accurate.", "Take a wrong turn again sometime, Jeongwoo.", "Walking out of the alley, I realized: there was no way I could take a wrong turn in a neighborhood I'd walked for ten years.", "Maybe my feet had read the bookmark in my wallet before I did.", "Chapter 11  The Person I Keep Running Into", "After that, I “took a wrong turn” once every two weeks.", "I had an excuse: picking out books to send Seoyun. Strangely, Seoyun asked for books more and more often.", "Then one Thursday night at eleven, my father called. My father is a man who almost never calls.", "Your mother fell in the yard. She's in the ER now. …Come quickly.", "My hands shook in the taxi. The first time since the night Seoyun had the flu.", "The ER corridor. Mother was sitting on a bed with a splint on her wrist. She looked better than I'd feared.", "Such a fuss. I just twisted my wrist a little. They say it's cracked.", "Cracked counts as “twisted a little”?", "Lend me one of your wrist braces. Mother and daughter— no, mother and son can match.", "If she was joking, she must be fine. Only then did I sink into a chair in the corridor. My legs gave out.", "Then I heard a familiar voice from the next seat.", "…Jeongwoo?", "Mustard cardigan. Round glasses. At this hour, in this hospital corridor.", "Jisoo, what are you doing here…", "One of my regulars, an old gentleman, collapsed in front of the bookstore. I said I'd stay until his family came. His daughter just arrived.", "And you? Your face is white.", "My mother fell. They say she's fine. She's fine, but… I'm not.", "Once I'd said it, my hands really were shaking. Jisoo got up without a word and went to the vending machine.", "When she came back, she was holding two paper cups.", "It's job's tears tea. They didn't have barley tea.", "The warmth of the tea reaches my hands.", "(Take it, and be honest) “Actually, I was scared.”", "After my daughter left, it's just my parents, and someday they'll… That was the first time the thought hit me.", "I lost my father three years ago. The corridor looked exactly like this.", "There was no one beside me then. That's why I'm sitting here now. Because I know what it means to have someone beside you.", "(Take it, and pretend you're fine) “Thank you. It's nothing.”", "People who are fine don't grip a paper cup that hard.", "I looked down. The cup was crushed. Sheepishly, I loosened my grip.", "I lost my father three years ago. The corridor looked exactly like this. So you don't have to pretend you're fine in front of me.", "We drank our tea in silence for a while. The vending machine's light fell in a square on the corridor floor.", "That makes three coincidences.", "Do you know what you call three?", "A reproducible bug.", "Is that the kind you fix, or the kind you leave alone?", "…Some bugs you want to leave alone.", "Having said it, my ears burned. Jisoo hid her mouth behind the paper cup, but her eyes were smiling.", "Then the curtain opened and Mother poked her head out.", "Jeongwoo, did you pay the— oh my, and who is this?", "Oh, well, she's someone I know, she runs a bookstore…", "This young lady smells of barley tea.", "…How did you know?", "I've brewed it for forty years, how could I not? Where do you get your barley?", "The two of them debated the origin of barley in the ER corridor for ten minutes. I stood between them holding the receipt.", "In the taxi home, Mother looked out the window and said:", "That young lady saw you shaking and went and got you tea. Didn't comfort you with words.", "People like that are rare. She's your kind.", "The next week, I didn't take a wrong turn. I walked straight to the bookstore. In my hand was a bag of barley.", "My mother asked me to give you this. It's the barley she uses.", "Your mother? Oh my goodness. How's her wrist?", "She put flower stickers on her cast. She told me to put some on too.", "Jisoo opened the bag, smelled it, and closed her eyes.", "This is really good barley. I should give something back.", "She took a thin picture book from under the counter, hesitated, and put it back.", "…No. This one's for later. I'll give you something else.", "What is it?", "My favorite book. I can't lend it to you yet. There's a right time for it.", "What I got instead was a new bookmark with the shop stamp. This time, an unsmudged “Comma Books.”", "I took the crumpled old bookmark out of my wallet and showed her.", "That's… the one I dropped that day? You kept that for three weeks?", "I meant to throw it away, but my wallet wouldn't let go.", "Blaming your wallet.", "She laughed, and I watched that laugh a little too long. Then our eyes met and we both turned toward the shelves.", "At the end of August, a typhoon came up.", "The company sent everyone home early. On my way to the bus, I stopped at the mouth of the alley.", "Comma Books was on a ground floor that was almost a half-basement. Jisoo had mentioned it in passing: when it rains hard, water comes over the threshold.", "She said that when it rains hard, water comes over the threshold.", "(Run into the alley)", "This time it wasn't a coincidence. On my own feet, knowing, I ran.", "(Hesitate. Are we close enough for me to go?)", "My feet wouldn't move. I was just a customer. Wouldn't it be a burden if I showed up?", "Then something fell over deeper in the alley. That was the end of thinking. I ran.", "The bookstore door was open. Jisoo was moving books from the bottom shelves up, alone. Water was already sloshing at ankle height on the floor.", "Jeongwoo?! What are you doing out in this—", "Bottom shelves first, right? I'll take this side.", "No explanation was needed. We carried books in silence, like that day at the bus stop. This time we both got soaked.", "When I set the last box on the counter, the lights went out. The whole neighborhood had lost power.", "…Of all times.", "In the dark came the sound of a match being struck. One candle, two, three. Then a camping lantern.", "In candlelight, the wet bookstore became somewhere else entirely. The gold lettering on the spines flickered.", "The buses will have stopped too. Stay until the rain lets up. The barley tea's on gas, so that still works.", "We sat facing each other on book boxes for chairs. Wet socks off, hot cups in hand.", "To be honest, I was a little scared today. This bookstore is everything I have.", "My ex-husband thought it was pathetic. Why hang on to something that doesn't make money? He said it when we split up too. “Go live with your books.”", "So I really did live with books. For five years.", "The candle was reflected twice in her glasses.", "The man who thought this bookstore was pathetic.", "“This bookstore is what's letting me breathe these days.”", "…No one's ever said that to me.", "It's true. Taking a wrong turn every two weeks is my only joy.", "So you admit now that they weren't wrong turns?", "…The bug reports piled up too high.", "“What's pathetic is his eyes.”", "I surprised myself. Had I ever judged someone so flatly?", "So you do get angry, Jeongwoo.", "For other people. Not so much for myself.", "I know. That's the kind of person you are.", "Now it was my turn. I told her about the three rejections. Being called heavy, the replies that stopped, and “you're too nice to give me butterflies.”", "Jisoo listened to the end, then looked at the candle for a long time.", "What are butterflies, anyway? I had huge butterflies for my ex-husband. He was always unpredictable. His promises, his moods.", "Looking back, those weren't butterflies. That was anxiety. Your heart races either way, so I mixed them up.", "When you see a man on a rainy day, getting soaked himself while holding his umbrella over someone else's books…", "She stopped. The candle flickered.", "…your heart beats differently. Without the anxiety.", "I couldn't find an answer. Outside, the typhoon was blowing, but in here it was strangely quiet.", "Jisoo reached under the counter. It looked like she was going for that thin book from before.", "Then the power came back. The fluorescent lights flickered on and the fridge began to hum.", "…Ah.", "She drew her hand back. She had the face of someone who'd missed something. I probably did too.", "At dawn, when the rain eased, Jisoo walked me to the mouth of the alley. The edge of the sky was turning a pale blue. She put the “Rental No. 1” umbrella in my hand.", "This time, make sure you use it. Over your own head.", "I will.", "Jeongwoo. Today wasn't a coincidence, was it?", "…It wasn't.", "Good. Three coincidences is enough. From now on, come on purpose.", "All the way home, I held the umbrella straight over my own head. It might have been the first time in forty-eight years.", "At the front door I checked my phone. There was a text from Seoyun.", "(text) Dad, are you ok in the typhoon? Did you eat?", "(reply) I had barley tea.", "(text) Barley tea? Did you go to Grandma's?", "(reply) No. A bookstore.", "(text) ???? Dad what did you just say?? A bookstore?? With who???", "I put the phone face down and laughed. I'd answer tomorrow.", "Chapter 12  The Thursday Person", "The day after the typhoon, Seoyun video-called the moment her class ended. Her eyes filled the screen.", "Bookstore. Barley tea. One in the morning. Explain.", "It was raining, so I helped move some books.", "Whose books.", "The bookstore owner's.", "Is the owner a grandpa or an auntie?", "…Someone who wears glasses.", "Dad, your ears are so red they're going off the screen.", "I tilted the camera. It didn't help. Seoyun had been an expert on my ears for fourteen years.", "What's her name?", "Yoon Jisoo.", "Seoyun froze on the screen for a moment. She had the face of someone remembering something. Then she shook her head.", "…Can't be. It's a common name.", "What can't?", "Nothing. Dad, what's she like?", "Seoyun asks about her.", "“She doesn't comfort you with words. She goes and gets you tea instead.”", "…So she's your kind.", "Your grandmother said the same thing.", "Grandma's already met her?! I'm the only one who hasn't! Not fair!", "“I don't know. She's just the bookstore owner.”", "You went through a typhoon to move books for “just the owner”? You?", "You're the guy who lets a bus go by when it rains. Because it's slippery.", "…That's true.", "Dad. I'm asking one thing.", "What.", "Don't get ready to be rejected. When something good happens, you always pack your bags first.", "That stung. I hadn't known my daughter knew that habit well enough to name it.", "…I'll try.", "Not try. Promise. Pinky.", "Seoyun put her pinky to the screen. So did I. Two fingers from across the sea met on the glass.", "The first Thursday of September. When I walked into the bookstore, Jisoo was white as a sheet.", "Jeongwoo, we're in trouble. Today's the picture book reading, and the person who was going to read lost her voice and can't come.", "Cushions were laid out on one side of the store. Six children and their mothers, and the old regular, were already seated.", "I could read, but I have to run things and handle the register too…", "Her eyes turned to me. I knew what they meant, and I wanted to pretend I didn't.", "I'm… someone who gets scolded in meetings for speaking too quietly.", "Didn't you read to your daughter when she was little?", "I did. Every night. Seoyun liked my voice because it made her sleepy.", "That's it. That's exactly the voice I need.", "Six children are waiting.", "“…I'll read just one.”", "Jisoo's face lit up. I hadn't agreed in order to see that face. …No, maybe a little.", "“The kids will be disappointed…”", "Disappointed is when the reading gets cancelled. Jeongwoo, please save me.", "I was weak to “save me.” For fourteen years I'd come running at those words.", "The book placed in my hands was that picture book. The grandmother brewing barley tea for her grandchild. The very book I'd picked and sent to Seoyun.", "I sat on a small chair in front of the cushions. Six children's eyes looked up at me. Scarier than six bugs.", "I turned the first page. My voice cracked. The child in front yawned.", "Then I remembered what Seoyun used to say when she was seven. “Dad, you have to do Grandma's voice like a grandma.”", "I cleared my throat and imitated my mother. “Such a fuss. Come here, your tea's getting cold.”", "The children laughed. The yawning child shuffled closer on her knees.", "When I closed the last page, the bookstore was quiet. Then the old regular started clapping.", "Well now, that voice is like barley tea. Nice and toasty.", "The mothers laughed, and the child in front put a book on my knee.", "Mister, one more.", "I read three books that day. Behind the counter, Jisoo sat with her chin in her hand, watching. Not running the register.", "Looks like I've found next month's reader.", "I said just once…", "The kids already call you “Mr. Barley Tea.” Once you've got a nickname, it's over.", "She'd said three coincidences was enough, but the world added one more.", "Tuesday lunch. I was waiting for the elevator with Minho in the lobby of our office building.", "The automatic doors opened, and someone came in carrying a box of books. Mustard cardigan.", "Oh? Jeongwoo?", "Jisoo, what are you…", "A delivery to the publisher on the ninth floor. Your company's in this building?", "The fourth floor. For fourteen years.", "I've been delivering to this building for two years.", "Two years. Two years of riding the same elevator and just missing each other.", "Beside me, Minho looked from my face to Jisoo's and back. A detective's eyes.", "Hello! I'm Park Minho, the boss's junior. Are you by any chance the Thursday—", "Minho.", "The boss has been leaving right on time every Thursday lately. It's a first since I joined, so the office formed an investigation committee.", "An investigation committee?", "There were three hypotheses. The night-class theory, the hospital theory, and the ro—", "Minho's third hypothesis.", "(Cover Minho's mouth)", "I clapped my hand over Minho's mouth. He was still laughing under my palm.", "I'm curious about the third hypothesis.", "It's been rejected.", "Too bad. That one sounded the most fun.", "“It's the bookstore. Investigation closed.”", "I said it without hiding anything. There was nothing to hide.", "Ooh… a confession straight from the boss.", "He's a regular at my bookstore. He does readings too.", "Readings?! The boss?! The man you can't hear even with a microphone in meetings?!", "The elevator arrived. I took Jisoo's box of books. It was heavier than I expected. She'd been carrying this alone for two years.", "Getting off at the ninth floor, Jisoo turned and said:", "See you Thursday. Give my regards to the investigation committee.", "When the doors closed, Minho leaned against the wall and looked at me.", "Boss.", "What.", "When you're with her, your shoulders are straight. I've worked under you for eight years and I've never seen that.", "I didn't answer. But the man in the elevator mirror really was standing with his shoulders straight.", "The last Thursday of September.", "Jisoo was wrestling with a notebook at the counter. A ledger where she wrote inventory by hand. Ten years' worth were piled up.", "To find how many copies of a book are left, I have to flip through all of these. Sometimes I take an order thinking we have it, then we don't, and I have to apologize…", "That I can help with.", "That weekend, I brought her an inventory spreadsheet. Type in a title and the quantity comes up, and when it drops below three copies, the cell turns yellow.", "This… turns yellow by itself?", "I made it barley-tea colored.", "Jisoo stared at the spreadsheet for a long time, then looked at me.", "Jeongwoo, why do you do things like this for me? All I've ever given you is barley tea.", "“Why do you do things like this for me?”", "“Because I want to. Is that not enough of a reason?”", "…It's enough. It's the best reason.", "She lowered her head and looked at the spreadsheet again. Her ears were a little red. I wasn't the only one whose ears went red.", "“I've received more than I've given. These days I look forward to Thursdays.”", "I surprised myself. It was the first time I'd said out loud what I'd only been thinking.", "…Me too. I roast the barley on Wednesday nights. So it's at its best on Thursday.", "In return, she taught me how to wrap books. The order for folding the corners, how to hide the tape.", "My hands were quick on a keyboard but clumsy with paper. Jisoo reached over my hand and held the corner for me.", "Her fingers touched the back of my hand. For about a second. We both pretended not to notice, and we both got the next corner wrong.", "That night, Jisoo walked with me to the bus stop for the first time. The spreadsheet had kept us late, so we closed up together.", "It was that bus stop. Where the books had spilled. The puddle on the paving stones was still there.", "This is where we first met.", "Yes. You were covering the books with your cardigan.", "And you were soaked, with your glasses all fogged up.", "Then raindrops began to fall. We both looked up at the sky and laughed. Whenever we came to this stop, it rained.", "Jisoo took Rental No. 1 out of her bag. This time, the two of us stood under one umbrella.", "Jisoo's bus came. The doors opened.", "Her bus has come.", "(Stay still. Don't tell her to get on)", "I didn't say “Your bus is here.” She didn't move either.", "“Your bus is here.”", "Yes. It is.", "But she didn't move.", "…I'll take the next one.", "The bus left. We waited for the next one under the umbrella.", "The next bus came. Jisoo looked at her watch.", "This one seems a bit early too.", "We let three go by. What we talked about wasn't much. How high to keep the flame when roasting barley, the difference between the hiragana ぬ and め, the photo of kimbap Seoyun had sent.", "The fourth bus was the last one. Only then did Jisoo move.", "Jeongwoo. Take the umbrella tonight. I'll be home in no time once I get off.", "That's the lie I told the first time.", "I know. Your bus that day was twenty minutes away. I checked the timetable at the stop.", "Caught. From the very start.", "That's why I went to that stop for three weeks. I wanted to know who gives away his umbrella with a lie.", "The doors of the last bus closed. Through the window, she waved. I stood holding the umbrella until the bus turned the corner.", "On the way home, I realized: looking forward to Thursdays was a dangerous sign.", "Once you have something to wait for, you have something to lose.", "(Seoyun. I promised, but it seems a habit of packing my bags isn't cured with one pinky.)", "Chapter 13  One Step Back", "Autumn came. Now I stopped by the bookstore every Thursday after work. On purpose, going straight there.", "Seoyun interrogated me over video call for three days. Name, age, the shape of her glasses, even how strong the barley tea was.", "(text) Dad, be good to her. And wait just a little. Something good is coming soon.", "(reply) What is?", "(text) Secret. I'm on deadline!", "Strange things began to happen at work too.", "Boss, you looked at my branch last night before you left, didn't you? The bug I couldn't catch for three days was fixed.", "I saw it in passing. It was nothing.", "I fixed the broken wheel on the building cleaner's cart, and bought dinner ahead of time for the youngest guy working late. Same as always.", "But lately, every time I did, people looked at me with an odd expression. As if holding back a smile, or checking something.", "We're playing futsal this weekend. Want to come? Everyone says they'd like you to.", "The leader of the next team went out of his way to buy me lunch, and a client's employee I'd never met asked, “Excuse me, are you Han Jeongwoo?” and shook my hand.", "Boss, why are you always doing other people's work?", "Because I'm done with mine.", "(quietly) …Exactly like the book.", "What?", "Nothing!", "(They've all got the wrong idea about something. They'll be disappointed when they find out what kind of person I am.)", "Still, that Thursday, I made a decision. I would ask her to have dinner somewhere other than the bookstore.", "I put on the shirt Minho had picked out again. I booked a restaurant. I even went ahead and memorized the menu.", "At the mouth of the alley, my steps stopped. Through the bookstore window, I could see two people.", "Jisoo, and a man I'd never seen. A tailored suit, a watch on his wrist, an imported car parked by the door.", "The man was holding Jisoo's hand and saying something. Jisoo's head was bowed.", "Voices leaked through the gap in the door.", "…So close this shop and let's start over. I'll take care of everything. I can't stand watching you struggle anymore.", "I didn't hear the rest. I didn't listen. My feet were already backing away.", "(Her ex-husband? …They look good together. That's the kind of man who suits her.)", "(And I'm the guy who holds out a three-thousand-won umbrella.)", "The two of them through the window.", "(Open the door and go in anyway)", "I took the handle. Just as the bell was about to ring, through the glass I saw Jisoo take the man's hand in hers.", "…That was as far as I got. My hand let go. I turned away before the bell could ring.", "I learned later: she hadn't been taking his hand. She'd been trying to pry it off.", "(Turn away)", "I quietly turned away. I called the restaurant to cancel. They said there was no fee.", "The old me who always got ready to be rejected first had, this time, stepped back before I was even rejected.", "I didn't go to the bookstore for three weeks after that.", "I ordered Seoyun's books online and sent them from the post office. The Rental No. 1 umbrella stayed propped by the front door.", "Two texts came from Jisoo. “Busy these days?” and “I roasted fresh barley.”", "All I replied was “Lots of work at the office.” It wasn't a lie. I'd made lots of work on purpose.", "(text) Dad, you didn't pick these books, did you?", "(reply) How did you know?", "(text) They don't smell. The ones you pick have a kind of smell. Did something happen?", "My daughter asks if something happened.", "(reply) “There's someone who suits her better.”", "(text) Did you see that? Did she say that?", "(reply) No. I could just see it.", "(text) Dad. When you code, do you fix bugs by guessing?", "(reply) …No. I look at the logs.", "(text) Then why do you jump to conclusions about people without looking at the logs.", "(reply) “It's nothing. I'm just busy.”", "(text) Your ears are red right now, aren't they.", "(reply) You can see my ears through a text?", "(text) I can. Dad, don't run away. With Mom, she was the one who left, but this time you're the one leaving.", "I stared at that text for a long time. A twenty-one-year-old knew me better than I did.", "On Friday of the fourth week, I went as far as the mouth of the alley. I still didn't have the courage to go in.", "But there was a sheet of paper on the bookstore window. I could read it from a distance.", "“Due to a rent increase, we will close at the end of next month. Thank you for everything. — Comma Books”", "My breath stopped. I heard that man's words from that day again. “Close this shop.”", "(So she's closing it. To start over with him.)", "(…No. This is the person who said on the night of the typhoon that this bookstore was everything she had.)", "Inside the window, Jisoo was putting price tags on books alone. Half-price stickers. Her shoulders looked small.", "Seoyun was right. I hadn't looked at the logs.", "The closing notice.", "(Go in and ask)", "I was about to push the door, then stopped. Going in empty-handed, what would I say? That I was sorry? To hang in there?", "I'm not good with words. There was only one thing I knew how to do.", "(First, do what you can)", "I'm not good with words. I wasn't confident I could go in and comfort her.", "But there was one thing I knew how to do.", "If the rent was the problem, then sales were the problem. The problem was that customers couldn't find a shop deep in an alley. That was a problem I could solve.", "From that night, I stayed at the office after work.", "An online ordering site for Comma Books. Carrying over the handwritten recommendation cards Jisoo used to write, just as they were, so anyone anywhere in the country could order.", "Office work by day, the site by night. It felt like going back to my freelancing days five years ago. Except I was five years older.", "Boss, you haven't gone home in three days, have you? What is this? Comma…?", "A hobby.", "Your hobby is overtime again. …This is that bookstore, isn't it? The one you went to every Thursday.", "Minho looked at the screen for a long while, then dragged a chair over and sat beside me.", "I'll handle hooking up payments. You get some sleep, Boss.", "At dawn on the fifth day, the last test passed. Green text came up on the screen.", "I stood up to go get coffee. The floor tilted. The monitor's glow drifted away.", "Boss? Boss!", "Minho's voice sounded like it was underwater. Then I heard nothing.", "When I opened my eyes, there was a white ceiling. An IV was in my arm.", "Overwork and dehydration. Minho told me later the doctor had said five all-nighters at my age was practically suicide.", "Someone was sitting by the bed. Mustard cardigan. She had taken off her glasses and was rubbing her eyes.", "…Jisoo?", "She raised her head. Her eyes were red.", "Minho called me. He said two of our bookstore's bookmarks came out of your wallet. Even the crumpled one.", "And he showed me this too.", "Jisoo held out her phone. On the screen was the site I'd built. “Comma Books — It's all right to take a wrong turn.”", "A man who wouldn't answer me for four weeks collapsed while making this?", "Jeongwoo, what is wrong with you… Why do you do things like this without saying anything?", "It was an angry voice. But tears kept falling from her eyes.", "“Why do you do things like this without saying anything?”", "“That day, in front of the bookstore… I saw you. With him.”", "That day? …You came that day?", "Yes. I'd even booked a restaurant. But I heard him say let's start over, and… I stepped back.", "“I'm not good with words. This is all I know how to do.”", "Then why didn't you come for four weeks? That's not about being bad with words.", "…That day, in front of the bookstore, I saw him. Saying let's start over. So I stepped back.", "Jisoo looked at me as if she couldn't believe it.", "That man is my ex-husband. He heard a rumor that I was closing the bookstore and came. To say “See, what did I tell you?”", "Do you know what he meant by starting over? Close the bookstore and come work as a bookkeeper at his company.", "I shook off his hand. And then I looked out the door… and there was nobody in the alley.", "That was the Thursday you were supposed to come. I'd roasted fresh barley tea that day.", "I had nothing to say. I was the man who'd taken down the server without even looking at the logs.", "I'm sorry. I… have a habit of getting ready to be rejected first.", "When something good happens, I think it must be some mistake. If I step back before the mistake gets corrected, it hurts less.", "And did it hurt less?", "…No. It hurt more.", "Jisoo looked at me for a long time, then laid her hand over the IV tape on the back of my hand. My bare wrist, its brace taken off, felt empty.", "I have conditions.", "Yes.", "No running away. When you're curious about something, ask instead of guessing. And no all-nighters.", "The third one's a bit hard, given my job…", "Then when you pull an all-nighter, report it.", "…Yes.", "Let's go slowly. I've been burned once too, so I can't go fast. But let's not go backward.", "Outside the window, it was getting light. Without letting go of my hand, Jisoo began to doze in her chair.", "I looked down at that hand. The hand that carried books, the hand that handed me a cup of tea.", "(Seoyun. This time Dad didn't run away. …To be exact, I ran away and got caught.)", "Chapter 14  New Project", "In the first week after the site opened, a hundred orders came in. Jisoo's handwritten recommendation cards were going around online.", "In the second week, three hundred. Jisoo hurt her wrist wrapping, and borrowed one of my braces.", "The “Closing” notice came off the window. A new one went up in its place. “We're staying open. Those who took a wrong turn are welcome.”", "The landlord wants to renew the lease. He'll even lower the increase. He says more people have been coming to this alley lately.", "That's great.", "So what's your fee, Mr. Developer?", "Unlimited barley tea.", "That was always unlimited.", "Then I've already been paid.", "Jisoo laughed, then glanced down under the counter once. Where that thin book was.", "…Just a little longer. I think the time is almost here.", "On Friday afternoon, the CEO called the whole staff into the meeting room.", "We've landed a big contract. We're turning an illustrated essay that sold three hundred thousand copies in Japan into a visual novel.", "The author named our company personally. Said she wouldn't do it anywhere else.", "Us? Why? We've never done a source that big.", "That's… something you'll understand when you see the original, Han.", "Everyone in the meeting room was looking at me and holding back smiles. The very same faces I'd been seeing for months.", "Friday night. With everyone gone home, Minho brought the source materials to me in the empty office.", "Boss. I think you should look at these materials yourself.", "Minho avoids my eyes. A thin book was set on my desk. Face down.", "I turned the book over.", "The cover. A drawing of a big hand holding a small one. A black brace on the wrist.", "The same composition as the drawing that had been stuck beside my monitor for fourteen years. Only the lines were the lines of a twenty-year-old.", "Title: <My Dad>. Words and pictures by Han Seoyun.", "…Seoyun?", "My hands were shaking so much the pages wouldn't turn.", "Episode 1, “Kimbap with Burst Sides.” Episode 2, “Dead-Last Dad.” Episode 3, “The Plate Outside the Door.” Episode 4, “A Hundred Backs.”", "At seven, I asked why Mom wasn't coming. Dad said he wasn't going anywhere. And he really didn't go anywhere.", "Dad's kimbap always had burst sides. Because he put in too much filling. I didn't understand that this was the shape of love until I was twenty.", "At sports day, Dad came in dead last. In dress shoes, smiling. I am the daughter of the man who ran the longest in the world.", "In the spring of fourth grade, I lied that Mom was busy. In the fall, Dad came to school wearing Grandma's flowered apron. Among all the moms, his songpyeon were the ugliest. I haven't lied since that day.", "In middle school, I closed my door. Every day, there was cut fruit outside it. That plate was the whole of my adolescence.", "Every weekend Dad secretly took freelance jobs. To pay for my academy. I pretended not to know, and drew a hundred pictures of his back instead.", "Dad backed up every one of my drawings. The file names were corny. I love that corny folder more than anything in the world.", "I was on every page. Me running last, me setting down the plate, me asleep at the table, me waving at the airport.", "Views of my back I had never once seen.", "And the last episode. Drawn in Kyoto, a story I didn't know.", "My dad has been dumped three times. They say he's too nice to give them butterflies.", "I feel sorry for the people who said that. The thing that gives you the most butterflies in the world is someone who's there in the same place every day.", "On rainy days, Dad brings only one umbrella. He forgets about his own. So I wish someone would hold an umbrella over my dad.", "The letters blurred and I couldn't read any more. The paper wasn't wet. My eyes were.", "Minho. Did you… know?", "We've all read it, in the original. The CEO bought it on a business trip to Japan and we passed it around. The main character's face is exactly yours.", "Even clients recognize you. As the real-life “Character No. 1.”", "So that's why everyone… lately, toward me…", "But it's not the book that made us like you. We read the book and looked at you again, and the book was all true.", "Secretly fixing bugs, buying the new guy dinner. We'd just been taking it for ten years.", "My hands shake as I hold my daughter's book.", "(Hold the book close)", "For the first time in forty-eight years, I cried out loud. I wasn't ashamed.", "Minho quietly pushed the whole box of tissues over, and turned off the meeting room lights.", "“…Let's set the schedule first. It's my daughter's work; there can't be any bugs.”", "I said that, but the text on the monitor blurred until I couldn't see a single line.", "Minho quietly pushed the whole box of tissues over.", "Boss. We'll set the schedule on Monday. Tonight, just cry.", "After a long while, I picked up the book again. And I realized something strange.", "This book. This size, this thickness. A thin, pretty Japanese book full of pictures.", "The rainy bus stop. The edge of the puddle. The last book, which I'd wiped with my sleeve and handed over face down.", "(No way.)", "I ran with the book. Eleven on a Friday night, to the alley behind the office.", "The bookstore lights were still on. Jisoo, holding packing tape, looked startled to see me, then saw the book in my hand and stopped.", "And slowly smiled. The face of someone who had waited a long time.", "The time has finally come.", "She took the thin book out from under the counter. The same cover. Only there was a water stain on the corner.", "This is the book you wiped with your sleeve at the bus stop that day. I couldn't get the stain out. I didn't get it out.", "Since when… did you know?", "At the bus stop I didn't. I just thought you were a strange man. Getting soaked himself, holding an umbrella over books.", "The second time, you told me. That your daughter studies art in Kyoto. That she'd drawn nothing but her dad's hands since she was seven.", "That night I reread this book. A dad who brings only one umbrella. A dad whose ears go red first. It all matched.", "Why didn't you tell me?", "“Why didn't you tell me?”", "“Did you like me… because I'm the man in the book?”", "After asking, I was afraid. If she said yes, all of this would be thanks to what my daughter drew.", "No. That's why I didn't tell you.", "(Wait in silence)", "I'd decided not to guess. I waited for the logs.", "…You don't guess anymore. You've learned well.", "Knowing you through a book felt like cheating. You didn't know anything about me, and I knew thirteen years of you.", "So I closed the book and decided to look at the person in front of me. The book might have been wrong. You might be that kind of dad only in your daughter's eyes.", "But your hands shaking in the ER, running over on the night of the typhoon, collapsing while making the site—none of that is in the book.", "I fell for the Jeongwoo who isn't in the book. The book was… just the trailer.", "She took a postcard from between the pages. It had a Japanese postmark.", "I was the first person in Korea to order this book. I loved it so much I sent the author a reader's postcard. And she wrote back.", "The postcard was in Seoyun's handwriting. Handwriting I had seen for fourteen years.", "“To my first reader in Korea. Thank you. If you ever see a man in Seoul with glasses and a wrist brace, please hold an umbrella over him. — Han Seoyun”", "…That kid.", "I couldn't do what she asked. Because you were the one who held the umbrella over me.", "So this isn't thanks to the author. It happened in reverse. The coincidence came first, and the book was the answer key I checked against later.", "I set the two books side by side. The stained one and the clean one. The same hands on the covers.", "At home I made a video call. Kyoto is in the same time zone. Seoyun picked up right away.", "Dad! That face… you found out? I was going to keep it secret until the game was announced!", "I insisted that if it was going to be a game, it had to be your company. The editors fought it hard, but I won.", "Dad, remember the promise we made at the East Sea? That I'd draw, and you'd build?", "…When you were twelve. With our pinkies.", "That's why I came to Kyoto, to keep it. Your dream was on standby, wasn't it? Standby's over now.", "Why didn't you tell me? For two years.", "You run away when you're praised. So I told three hundred thousand people first, so you couldn't run.", "Dad, you're not someone who never got chosen. I chose you every day. Even if I were born again, I'd be your daughter.", "I know you think you couldn't make up for Mom's share. But Dad, I never went short. It was a double serving.", "“I never went short.”", "“Dad… was loved.”", "You only just figured that out? So slow. It took you fourteen years.", "“Thank you. And I'm sorry I didn't see it.”", "Leave out the sorry. I'll just take the thank you.", "And Seoyun. I saw the postcard. The one you sent your first reader.", "Huh? How did you… wait. Wait a second. The bookstore. Barley tea. Don't tell me the bookstore owner is…", "Yoon Jisoo.", "Seoyun screamed on the screen. Someone banged on the wall from the next dorm room.", "You and the woman I asked to hold an umbrella over you?! Even if I drew this, my editor would say it's too contrived!", "I was the one who held the umbrella.", "Of course you were. You're Dad. Oh, I really… Dad, can I cry?", "Cry. I was crying until just now too.", "Are your ears red?", "Very.", "That night I rolled kimbap by myself. For the first time since Seoyun left. The sides burst again. I took a photo and sent it to her.", "(text) Same as ever. That's why I love it.", "From that day on, I straightened my shoulders in front of the mirror. What had changed wasn't the world. It was the way I saw myself.", "Chapter 15  What the Book Left Out", "The week after I read the book was strange. The world was the same, but the ground I walked on felt different.", "I read <My Dad> seven times. Each time, I stopped somewhere different.", "But on the seventh time I noticed: there was one thing missing from this book. What I like.", "Seoyun had drawn everything I did. Making kimbap, running, setting down the plate. But not a single line about my favorite food, my favorite color, what I'd like to do on a day off.", "Seoyun hadn't left it out. There was nothing to draw. In fourteen years, I had never chosen any of those things.", "(What do I like?)", "I opened the fridge. Seoyun's favorite yogurt was still there out of habit. There wasn't a single thing I'd bought because I liked it.", "On the first Tuesday of December, a text came from an unknown number. “It's Minkyung. I read the book. Could I see you, just once?”", "Minkyung. Seoyun's mother. That month, the Korean edition of Seoyun's book had come out, and it sat in the middle of the display tables in bookstores. With the hand drawing on the cover.", "Sleet clung to the window of the café where we'd agreed to meet. Neither snow nor rain, it scraped down the glass like on the night Seoyun had the flu.", "She was there first. The first time in fourteen years since the courthouse.", "It's been a long time. …Is your wrist still like that?", "It's both of them now.", "She put the book on the table. The band around it read: “Three hundred thousand readers cried.”", "When I met Seoyun, all I did was tell her I was sorry. I didn't ask a single thing about you.", "I thought I had no right to ask. But after reading this, I understood. It wasn't about the right. I was scared. Scared to find out how much you'd done alone.", "So I came to apologize. Not to Seoyun. To you.", "I didn't leave back then because you didn't give me butterflies. I ran away. Because raising a child scared me. You didn't seem scared at all, and that scared me even more.", "The answer I'd been calculating as my own fault for fourteen years turned into a different number on the table.", "An apology, fourteen years in coming.", "“…I was scared too. Every day. It just didn't show.”", "…I see. I didn't know that either.", "Seoyun knew. From my ears going red.", "“I accept your apology. But apologize to Seoyun first, properly.”", "I did. In a letter. There's been no… reply yet.", "She's slow. She takes after me. Whether she replies or not, that's hers to decide.", "She nodded. Like letting out a breath held for a long time.", "You look happy. Even more than in the book.", "A little, these days. I'm learning late.", "We each paid for our own. That seemed right.", "When we stepped out of the café, the sleet had thickened. She had no umbrella. I took two umbrellas out of my bag.", "Take one. My daughter told me to pack two. Mine, and one for someone else.", "…You haven't changed.", "No. Before, I'd have brought just one, given it to you, and walked home in the rain. This time I have one for myself too.", "We each opened our own umbrella and walked in opposite directions. Something that was neither resentment nor longing melted on the paving stones with the sleet.", "A door I'd kept half open for fourteen years finally closed, without a sound.", "That Thursday, I thought about rebooking the restaurant I'd cancelled before, and decided not to.", "Instead, I made kimbap. Not at dawn but after work, slowly. The sides still burst.", "After closing the bookstore, Jisoo turned off the fluorescent lights and brought out the candles from the night of the typhoon.", "Is this the kimbap? From Episode 1?", "Yes. Rather shabby for something three hundred thousand people read about.", "Jisoo picked up a burst one first. She chewed it for a long time and closed her eyes.", "…The feature beats the trailer.", "The rice is mushy. And the pickled radish is sticking out.", "Jeongwoo. When someone compliments you, just take it. Don't answer with a bug report.", "How to take a compliment.", "“…Thank you. I'm glad you like it.”", "That's it. That's all you have to do.", "Receiving was harder than giving. But having done it once, the second time seemed like it might be a little easier.", "“Still, next time I'll try to make them burst less.”", "No. They have to burst to be your kimbap. If they don't, they're counterfeit.", "She picked up another burst kimbap. With the face of a counterfeit inspector.", "When the candle had burned halfway, I brought up my ex-wife. Even that I'd met her that week. A story I'd never told anyone all the way through.", "When she left, I didn't try to stop her. I didn't even get angry.", "Everyone says it's because I'm nice, but it isn't. I… thought it was only natural. That someone like her would never stay long beside someone like me.", "So before it was her fault for leaving, I'd been living with the door half open from the start. So it would be easy to go.", "Jisoo listened without a word. The candle flickered in her glasses.", "Are you keeping that door open with me too?", "I took one breath before answering. No guessing. No running away.", "“Are you keeping that door open with me too?”", "“…I'm closing it. It's stiff, so it's taking a while.”", "Then I'll push from inside. Let's close it together.", "She really did hold out both hands and pretend to push a door in the air. I laughed, and my throat tightened.", "“I thought leaving it open was the polite thing. So you could leave anytime.”", "That's not politeness. That's insurance. I can't be with someone who's taken out insurance.", "Her voice trembled for the first time. Only then did I understand: a half-open door makes the person inside cold too.", "…I'll close it. Even if it's stiff and takes a while.", "I have a confession too. When someone is good to me, I get scared.", "My ex-husband was good to me at first too. Then one day he started pulling out everything he'd done for me like a ledger. “After all I've done for you.”", "So when you built that site for me, I was as scared as I was happy. Scared that you'd pull out a ledger someday too.", "I don't keep ledgers.", "I know. Someone who did wouldn't write things like “Looks like a pig but the artist insists it's a rabbit” on fourteen years of drawing files.", "We laughed. We talked until the candle burned out. When we laid out our fears, they turned out to be about the same size.", "That Sunday, Seoyun made an announcement.", "Three-way call. Dad, go to the bookstore.", "I propped my phone up on the bookstore counter. Seoyun on the screen and Jisoo off it faced each other for the first time.", "Hello. I'm Han Seoyun. The author, and this man's guardian.", "Hello. I'm Yoon Jisoo. The first reader, and in charge of this man's barley tea.", "For about three seconds they sized each other up. I stood between them like someone waiting for interview results.", "I'll ask just three questions. Number one. When do my dad's ears turn red?", "When he's praised, when he's lying, and right before he cries. The color's slightly different for each.", "…Number two. When Dad says “It's nothing,” what does it mean?", "That it's something. He stayed up all night, or secretly helped someone.", "Number three. What's Dad's favorite food?", "Jisoo stopped. She looked at me. I didn't know the answer either.", "…That I don't know yet. I don't think he knows either.", "On the screen, Seoyun slowly nodded.", "You pass. Number three was a trap. If you'd pretended to know, you'd have failed.", "Nobody knows that. Not even me. Dad always says “Anything's fine.” It's the only thing I couldn't draw.", "So… please find that out for me. I'm far away now.", "Seoyun's voice wavered a little at the end. Jisoo moved closer to the screen.", "I'll find out. And when I do, I'll report to the author first. So you can put it in Volume 2.", "It's a promise. And… can I call you “auntie”? “Ms. Owner” feels too far away.", "Of course. And can I call you just Seoyun?", "Yes. …Dad, why are you crying? Your ears are the third color.", "The trouble was that “number three.”", "After that, Jisoo kept asking me. What do you want to eat, where do you want to go, what color do you like.", "Every time, I gave the same answer.", "Anything's fine. Whatever you like.", "On the third Thursday, Jisoo put down the tape she was packing with.", "Jeongwoo, I'm a little angry.", "What? What did I…", "I don't want to hear “Anything's fine.” That's not being considerate. It's dumping every decision on me.", "And I'm curious about you, but you keep trying to show me only myself. I'm not dating a mirror.", "It was a kind of anger I'd never heard. She was the first person ever to be angry because I accommodated her too much.", "She says she doesn't want to hear “Anything's fine.”", "“…I really don't know. In fourteen years, I've never chosen.”", "It wasn't an excuse; it was the truth. Some of the anger left Jisoo's face.", "Then start choosing now. It's fine to get it wrong. If you choose something and it's not good, you just choose something else next time.", "“I'm sorry. From now on, I'll decide.”", "I'm not asking you to be sorry. And I'm not asking you to “decide for me” either. I'm asking you to say what you want.", "It took me a long time to understand the difference. Deciding for someone, and wanting something. All my life I'd only done the first.", "Homework. By next Thursday, decide on one thing you want to eat. Not “something Jisoo might like.”", "That week was harder than a launch week. Every lunch, I froze in front of the menu. Enough that Minho got worried.", "Boss, why are you looking at the menu like it's a code review?", "Minho. How do you know what you like?", "You eat it and you know. …Boss, don't tell me you don't know that?", "On Wednesday night, on my way home, my feet stopped in front of a street stall. A steaming pot. The smell of fish cake broth.", "What I used to eat standing alone after working late, in college, before Seoyun was born. Something I'd forgotten for twenty years.", "The next day, the moment I walked into the bookstore, I said it. Before even saying hello.", "Fish cakes. Eaten standing at a street stall. With the broth in a paper cup.", "Jisoo blinked, then broke into a wide smile. She turned off the bookstore lights and threw on her cardigan.", "Let's go. Now.", "A street stall in December. We stood side by side eating fish cakes. Our breath mixed with the steam from the broth.", "I ate seven skewers. Jisoo was so busy watching me eat that she only had three.", "It's the first time I've seen you really enjoy eating something. You're always looking after my food first, so you only ever eat it cold.", "This is… something I chose.", "Remember that face. It's the face you make when you've chosen what you want. That's the face I like best.", "Jisoo took out her phone and typed something. A text to Seoyun.", "(text) Dear author, I found the answer to number three. Fish cakes. Street stall. Broth in a paper cup.", "The reply came in ten seconds. Exclamation marks filled the screen.", "(text) !!!!!!! Volume 2, Episode 1, confirmed!!!!", "On the way back, it started to snow. The first snow.", "We shared Rental No. 1. Our shoulders touched, but neither of us moved away.", "Jeongwoo. Can I ask you something? Asking, not guessing.", "Yes.", "What are we?", "My steps stopped. It was so quiet I could hear the snow piling up on the umbrella.", "I knew the answer. I just didn't want to say it in the back alley of a street stall, with no preparation. This time, I wanted to do it properly.", "“What are we?”", "“I want to answer that properly. Please wait until spring.”", "Spring? Why spring?", "I think that's how long it'll take for the door to close all the way. I don't want to say it with the door half shut.", "…All right. Until spring. Not one day more.", "“…My heart's made up. I just need a little more courage to say it.”", "How much more?", "Until spring. Then I'll say it so you won't need to guess.", "Until spring. Not one day more.", "Under the umbrella, she held out her pinky. Like the finger Seoyun had pressed to the screen.", "I hooked my pinky around hers. This time, with no glass between us.", "That winter, for the first time, I waited for spring. Waiting for something no longer scared me.", "Chapter 16  The Kamo River", "These days, the person who laughs loudest at Studio Moonlight is me.", "Boss, please stop whistling. It's louder than the keyboards.", "Development of the game <My Dad> began. I found myself doing the strange job of turning my own life into code.", "Boss, this choice here. “Dad makes kimbap / Dad buys kimbap.” Which did you actually pick?", "…I agonized over both. So we're putting in both. Whichever you pick, you end up taking out the seaweed at four in the morning.", "Then the choice doesn't mean anything.", "It does. The agonizing stays with you. People are remembered less for the result than for the time they spent hesitating.", "…You've gotten a lot more talkative lately, Boss.", "In March, the CEO called me in.", "Han, go to Kyoto for a bit. A meeting with the author, plus a signing to announce the game adaptation. The author insists we send her father.", "I've never once been abroad.", "You're going to see your daughter, what's scary about that? You have a passport, right?", "I had a passport. One I'd made just in case when Seoyun left the country, without a single stamp in it.", "On Thursday, I brought up the business trip at the bookstore.", "I'm going to Kyoto next week. For five days.", "Oh, how wonderful. You'll be seeing your daughter for the first time in two years, won't you?", "I wanted to… ask you to come with me. But it's a business trip.", "I'm a bit busy next week too. I have some books to bring in.", "She smiled oddly as she poured the barley tea. I wouldn't understand that smile until five days later.", "Pack two umbrellas. They say Kyoto gets a lot of spring rain.", "My daughter said exactly the same thing.", "Good advice comes from more than one person.", "Kansai Airport. At the first immigration check of my life, I froze when asked the purpose of my visit.", "Uh… daughter. My daughter. Character… number one.", "The officer smiled and stamped my passport. The first stamp in it.", "When the arrival gates opened, someone with an art bag on her shoulder was standing there holding a sign.", "A sign written in crayon. “My Dad = Character No. 1.” The sign from sports day. Kept for thirteen years.", "Dad!", "Seoyun came running. In two years her hair had grown a little longer, and the faded yellow hairpin was still in her bangs.", "I dropped my bag and opened my arms. Seoyun crashed into them.", "Dad, you've lost weight. Did you eat?", "I had two in-flight meals. You?", "I couldn't eat since this morning because you were coming.", "In the middle of the airport, in tears, we traded a greeting thirteen years old.", "Seoyun's dorm room was six tatami mats. One whole wall was drawings.", "My hands, my back, my ears. And drawings I'd never seen. A man tilting his umbrella at a rainy bus stop.", "This… when did you draw it?", "Auntie Jisoo told me on a postcard. That you gave her your whole umbrella and left. I heard that and drew all night.", "You two talk about things like that?", "We're the author and her first reader, of course we do. We talk about you behind your back, too.", "They say the two of them talk about me behind my back.", "“…What do you say about me?”", "That you reply to texts in three seconds, but go four weeks without saying the things that actually matter.", "…I fixed that.", "I know. I heard about that too. The No Running Away Treaty.", "“I'm glad you two get along.”", "Dad, honestly, I was scared. That if someone came to be beside you, my place would get smaller.", "But she doesn't touch my place. She's the kind of person who sets one more chair beside it.", "On the desk were a bamboo mat and seaweed for kimbap. Seoyun laughed, embarrassed.", "I roll them here too. They still burst. My Japanese friends think that's the Korean style.", "The next day, a bookstore in central Kyoto. A signing that doubled as the announcement of the game.", "Seoyun put me not in the reserved seats, but at the very end of the regular readers' line.", "You come through the line. As a reader. That was my wish.", "The line stretched out of the store into the alley. People standing with my daughter's book held to their chests. Students in uniform, a mother with a baby, a white-haired old man.", "The young Japanese man in front of me glanced at me, then looked from the book cover to my wrist and back. And his eyes went wide.", "I couldn't catch what he shouted. I only heard the word “otōsan,” and “kyarakutā ichiban.”", "The whole line turned around. Applause broke out. Someone was crying. People held out their books and pointed at my hands.", "(These people… know me. My kimbap, my last-place run, my plate.)", "The people in line are clapping.", "(Bow clumsily)", "I bowed ninety degrees. I didn't know any Japanese, so I spoke in Korean. “Thank you. For reading my daughter's book.”", "No interpreter was needed. An old man came up and wrapped my hand in both of his.", "(Freeze)", "My legs locked. I'd never been applauded, so I didn't even know how to stand.", "Then an old man came up and wrapped my hand in both of his. Only then could I breathe.", "The old man said one thing in halting Korean. He seemed to have practiced it.", "Good… father.", "Words no one had ever said to me in forty-nine years. Instead of answering, I only nodded. My ears felt like they were on fire.", "The line shrank, and the signing table drew closer. Then someone tapped me on the shoulder from behind.", "Excuse me, is this the end of the line?", "I turned around. Mustard cardigan. Round glasses. In her arms, a book with a water stain on the corner.", "…Jisoo?! How are you here—", "I told you I had books to bring in. The Kyoto book fair. And the author sent me an invitation to the signing.", "So from the start…", "You have no idea how hard I tried not to laugh when you told me your trip dates.", "I looked toward the table. Seoyun, felt-tip pen in hand, was looking our way and grinning. The face of the culprit.", "So this isn't a coincidence.", "No. This time it's on purpose. The author and I planned it.", "It was our turn. Seoyun took Jisoo's stained book first.", "My first reader. At last we meet. I hear the umbrella request didn't work out.", "No. Your father beat me to it.", "I knew he would. That's why I like him.", "Seoyun signed the book, and drew a picture beside it. One umbrella. Two people beneath it.", "And in my book she wrote: “To Character No. 1. Now it's your turn, Dad. — Your daughter”", "Evening. Seoyun took us to the bank of the Kamo River. The river I'd only seen in photos for two years.", "The cherry blossoms were falling. One by one, the lanterns of the riverside restaurants came on.", "Oh, right. I have a meeting with my editor. An hour… no, about two hours.", "Now? All of a sudden?", "Yep. Very sudden. You two sit here. That rock right there is the best spot.", "Seoyun made a fist where only I could see it, and ran off. Her art bag bounced on her back.", "We sat side by side on the rocks of the bank. About a hand's width apart. The water murmured.", "Your daughter's good at directing.", "I don't know who she gets it from.", "From you. Setting the stage without saying a word.", "For a while we both just looked at the river. My hand and hers lay on the rock, a hand's width apart.", "(I have to say it. Without guessing, without stepping back.)", "The old me, the one who got ready to be rejected first, raised his head. “It must be a mistake. Something this good would never come to you.”", "For the first time, I answered that voice. It's not a mistake. The people in line today, the postcard, the stained book—they're the proof.", "A hand's width apart. Say it, or not?", "“Jisoo. I'm not someone who gives people butterflies. But still…”", "Say that one more time and I'll push you in the river.", "…What?", "I've got butterflies right now. For a year now. So saying you're not someone who gives butterflies is rude to my heart.", "“Jisoo. I like you. I'm asking, not guessing. How do you feel about me?”", "Having said it, I held my breath. The longest three seconds of my forty-nine years.", "…Want to see the logs?", "Yes.", "I have butterflies right now. For a year now. Since the bus stop.", "(Fail to say it in the end)", "My mouth opened and closed. Three times. The sound of the river covered my turn.", "…The river's beautiful. It really is the best spot, like your daughter said.", "Yes. …It is the best spot.", "She moved her hand. The hand's width was gone. On the back of my hand rested the hand that had carried books.", "Let me ask too. I'm a person who has nothing but a bookstore. I've failed once, and I scare easily. Is that all right?", "That bookstore is how I breathe. And I've failed too. As for being scared… let's be scared together.", "Then a drop of water fell on my forehead. Kyoto spring rain.", "I dug through my bag. Two people had told me to pack two umbrellas, and once again I hadn't packed even one.", "I knew it.", "Jisoo took an umbrella out of her bag. A three-thousand-won plastic umbrella. With a ribbon tied on the handle: Rental No. 1.", "She opened the umbrella and held it over my head. Not noticing that her own shoulder was getting wet.", "I'm finally doing what the author asked.", "“If you ever see a man in Seoul with glasses and a wrist brace, please hold an umbrella over him.”", "I took the handle and tilted it toward her. She tilted it back toward me. In the end we had no choice but to sit pressed close together.", "On the bridge across the river, someone was looking our way and sketching furiously in a sketchbook. With an art bag on her shoulder.", "So much for a meeting with her editor.", "I guess that was her deadline. The cover of Volume 2.", "She looked only at the river for a long time. Her hand on the rock didn't move.", "Thank you, Jeongwoo. Truly.", "But… I still don't really know you. You were always one step behind. At the bookstore, at the bus stop, in that alley.", "That one step made me stand just as far away. So let's go a little slower. I'm not saying let's go backward.", "Slowly. I retranslated that word in my head over and over. Did it mean no? Did it mean a mistake?", "Then a drop of water fell on my forehead. Kyoto spring rain. Jisoo took out Rental No. 1 and held it over my head.", "I have to do what the author asked. She asked me to hold an umbrella over you.", "We sat under one umbrella. The hand's width between us stayed. On the bridge across the river, someone slowly lowered a sketchbook.", "Then a drop of water fell on my forehead. Kyoto spring rain.", "I dug through my bag. Two people had told me to pack two umbrellas, and once again I hadn't packed even one.", "I knew it.", "Jisoo took out Rental No. 1 and held it over my head. Not noticing that her own shoulder was getting wet.", "I'm finally doing what the author asked. If I ever saw a man in Seoul with a wrist brace, she asked me to hold an umbrella over him.", "I said thank you. That was all I could say. What I wanted to say flowed away below my throat, like the river.", "On the bridge across the river, someone slowly lowered a sketchbook. With an art bag on her shoulder.", "Chapter 17  Five Days in Kyoto", "The next morning, Seoyun's dorm room. Three people sat in a circle on six tatami mats.", "Seoyun folded her arms and looked from one of us to the other. Like a detective in an interrogation room.", "I saw everything from the bridge last night. You two sitting pressed together under one umbrella. So what's the verdict?", "…I told her.", "Told her what.", "That I like her.", "Seoyun sat still for about three seconds, then flopped flat on the tatami and kicked her feet.", "Dad! Said it! With his own mouth! Without guessing! This is Volume 3 material!", "Volume 2 isn't even out yet, author.", "I'm behind. The material's piling up too fast.", "Seoyun sprang up and brought the bamboo mat from her desk.", "Celebration slash breakfast. Each of us rolls one. It's the entrance exam for our family, Auntie.", "Mine burst. Seoyun's burst too. Naturally.", "Jisoo's kimbap… didn't burst. Neat and firm, rolled like something out of a textbook.", "…Auntie, this is cheating.", "I guess ten years of wrapping books did that to my hands.", "Her kimbap didn't burst.", "“It's fine. Someone has to make ones that don't burst, so we can pack lunches.”", "Ooh, Dad took her side. Already.", "Then I'll put in more filling next time. Until it bursts.", "Jisoo got greedy with her second roll on purpose, and the sides burst for all to see. Seoyun applauded.", "“You fail. It goes against our family motto.”", "What's the family motto?", "Burst or not, it tastes the same.", "Then I'll try again. Until it bursts.", "Jisoo got greedy with her second roll on purpose, and the sides burst for all to see. Seoyun applauded.", "You pass. Love is putting in too much filling. I wrote that in Volume 1, Episode 1.", "We shared three rolls of burst kimbap. Sunlight stretched long across the tatami room.", "The next morning, Seoyun's dorm room. She said Jisoo had left early for the first day of the book fair.", "Seoyun folded her arms and looked at me. Like a detective in an interrogation room. Only today, a slightly tired detective.", "I saw everything from the bridge last night. Under one umbrella, a hand's width apart. So what's the verdict?", "…I told her. That I like her.", "Really? Then why were you sitting like that?", "She said let's take our time getting to know each other.", "Seoyun looked at me for a long time, then plopped down on the tatami.", "Dad. That's not a rejection. Your translator's broken again.", "…Is it?", "Yes. Slowly means slowly. It doesn't mean no.", "…I couldn't say it.", "Your mouth opening and closing three times—I saw it all from the bridge.", "Dad, I wrote postcards for a year to set this up.", "I'm sorry.", "I told you to leave out the sorry. …No, this time I'll take it. But it's Auntie you should be sorry to, not me.", "Seoyun brought the bamboo mat from her desk.", "Let's just have breakfast. One roll each. As consolation.", "Mine burst. Seoyun's burst too. Naturally.", "Burst or not, it tastes the same. Our family motto. …Dad, live by the motto.", "Sunlight stretched long across the tatami room. We shared the burst kimbap in silence.", "On the third day, I went to Seoyun's school. Her advisor had said he really wanted to meet me.", "The night before, I practiced one sentence a hundred times. Mustering everything I'd learned from the hiragana chart on the fridge.", "The professor had white hair and smiling eyes. The moment he saw me, he looked at my wrist. Then he said something to Seoyun.", "The professor says you kept the promise from the interview. He said “Show me that character when it's finished,” and I really brought him.", "Character? That's… me?", "Smiling, the professor took an old folder from a drawer. The interview record. And he began to read it slowly in Japanese.", "Seoyun's face went red. She tried to stop interpreting, but the professor waved her on. With a resigned face, she began.", "…“Since I was seven, I have drawn one person's hands. My father's hands.”", "“My father doesn't know how to look at art, but he kept every one of my drawings. I want to draw people like that. People no one draws as the main character.”", "“I heard this school teaches character. I want to turn my father into a character that people all over the world love.”", "It was the answer I never got to hear at the front door two years ago. In a Kyoto office, I heard why “otōsan” had come up four times.", "Dad, I was going to tell you this myself. The professor beat me to it.", "The professor closed the folder and spoke to me again, slowly. Seoyun translated.", "“Your daughter was not an exceptionally talented student.”", "My heart sank. Seoyun went on interpreting, expressionless.", "“Her lines are slow, and she can't draw in the fashionable styles. But this student has looked at one person for fifteen years.”", "“A picture isn't drawn with the hand. It's drawn with the time you've spent looking. It was you who gave her that time. Thank you.”", "The professor bowed. Flustered, I bowed even deeper. Then I brought out the sentence I'd practiced.", "Musume o… yoroshiku… onegaishimasu. (Please take care of my daughter.)", "My pronunciation must have been a mess. The professor smiled and took my hand. Seoyun turned her head and looked at the ceiling.", "Your pronunciation dried up my tears instantly. Thanks.", "On the way back, Seoyun said out of nowhere:", "Dad, how did you feel when he said “not talented”?", "The words “not talented.”", "“I was angry. But when I heard it to the end, it was praise.”", "Right? I cried at first too. But it's the best thing anyone's ever said to me.", "Talent is mine, but the time spent looking belongs to both of us.", "“I knew from the start. You're not a kid who draws with talent.”", "What, you thought so too?", "I've seen everything since v001. You didn't draw because you were good at it. You drew because you couldn't stand not to.", "…That's going in the book.", "The fourth night. Jisoo went to the book fair after-party, and Seoyun and I sat by the river, just the two of us.", "Seoyun had bought fish cakes from the convenience store. She said she'd gotten Jisoo's report.", "Dad, there's something I didn't put in the book.", "Okay.", "In my first year, I almost quit. In the summer.", "My hand holding the fish cake stopped. That summer, Seoyun had sent photos every week. The river, a window, pencil shavings. With smiling emojis.", "I couldn't communicate. I couldn't understand half the lectures, and everyone drew better than me. More days than not, I cried while eating in the dorm.", "I even looked up plane tickets. One-way.", "Why didn't you tell me?", "That was tuition you paid with freelance work. That was Grandpa's savings. How could I say I was quitting after three months?", "So every Sunday I picked the photo that looked the most okay and sent it. With “Dad, did you eat?”", "I looked at the river. On those Sundays, I'd boiled ramen in a pot, photographed it and sent it. To look as okay as possible.", "We both only sent okay-looking photos.", "“…I lied too. More days than not, I didn't eat.”", "I know.", "You knew?", "The ramen photos were always the same pot at the same angle. Dad, you took it once and reused it.", "Caught. After two years. We'd been sending each other only our most okay-looking photos, and failing to fool each other.", "“If you'd told me, I'd have come right away.”", "That's why I didn't. You really would have come. Even if it meant ditching your job.", "And you lied too. The ramen photos were always the same pot at the same angle. You took it once and reused it.", "Caught. After two years. We'd been sending each other only our most okay-looking photos, and failing to fool each other.", "That night, when I was about to buy the ticket, I opened my sketchbook instead. Because I missed home. I drew your hands. Then kimbap. Then sports day.", "I kept drawing, and it was morning. There were twelve pages. I just posted them online. With clumsy Japanese captions.", "When I woke up, there were three thousand comments. “My dad was like this too.” “My dad wasn't like this, I'm jealous.” “Is this dad a real person?”", "That's when I knew why I'd come here. Not to draw the fashionable stuff. I came to draw you.", "Seoyun hugged her knees. The lanterns across the river wavered on the water.", "So Dad, that book isn't a present I gave you. You saved me. Again.", "I pulled Seoyun's shoulder toward me. At twenty-two her shoulders were broader than at seven, but they still fit inside my arm.", "Next time, tell me. Send the not-okay photos too, not just the okay ones.", "You too.", "…Yes. Me too.", "We hooked pinkies. This time, without a screen.", "The last night. In the dorm's shared kitchen, I was chopping ingredients for the kimbap I'd feed them in the morning.", "Seoyun's and Jisoo's voices drifted out of the room. I didn't mean to eavesdrop. My knife was just quiet.", "Auntie. Can I ask you one thing?", "Of course.", "Please don't… leave my dad. Even if he's left, he doesn't get angry. He thinks it's because he isn't enough. That scares me the most.", "The knife stopped.", "Seoyun. I've been left too. I know what it's like.", "So here's the promise I'll make. Not a promise never to leave—a promise that when I'm angry I'll show it, and when I'm hurt I'll say so.", "If no one tells your dad, he counts everything as his own fault. So I'll leave him logs. Every time.", "It was quiet for a long while. Then I heard Seoyun sniffling.", "…Auntie, you've caught my dad's way of talking. “Logs.”", "Of course I have. I've been listening to it for a year.", "I stood at the cutting board for a long time. The pickled radish mustn't get wet with tears.", "Kansai Airport. This time, I was the one leaving.", "Seoyun held out a lunch bag. Kimbap she'd gotten up alone at dawn to make.", "Eat it on the plane. Both of you. The burst ones are Dad's, the less burst ones are Auntie's.", "They're all burst.", "So share them nicely.", "The boarding announcement sounded. Two years ago at Incheon, I was the one who stayed, and Seoyun went in without looking back.", "This time I was the one walking. Side by side with Jisoo. At the gate, I stopped.", "At the gate.", "(Look back)", "I looked back. I knew I'd cry if I did, but crying didn't scare me anymore.", "(Walk on without looking back, like Seoyun)", "I tried not to look back. But Jisoo caught my sleeve and turned me around.", "Look. Your daughter's waiting.", "Seoyun was still standing right where we'd left her. Holding the thirteen-year-old sign above her head.", "“My Dad = Character No. 1.” Underneath, new letters had been added. “+ No. 2 (in charge of barley tea).”", "Seoyun was laughing as she cried. This time she didn't hide it. Her not-okay face, just as it was.", "Dad! Eat! For real!", "You too! For real!", "We shouted loud enough to shake the airport. People stared. My ears felt like they were burning, but I didn't care.", "On the plane we shared the burst kimbap. Looking at the clouds outside the window, Jisoo said:", "You have a good daughter.", "Yes. And she picked out a good person for me. By postcard.", "I keep telling you, it happened in reverse. The coincidence came first.", "I know. But I want to say thank you to both of them anyway.", "Kansai Airport. This time, I was the one leaving. Jisoo still had book fair business and would come back a day later.", "Seoyun held out a lunch bag. Kimbap she'd gotten up alone at dawn to make.", "Eat it on the plane. They all burst.", "Same as ever.", "Dad. When you get back to Seoul, pack two umbrellas. One for you, and one for… the person you'll hold it over.", "The boarding announcement sounded. Two years ago at Incheon, I was the one who stayed, and Seoyun went in without looking back.", "At the gate.", "(Look back)", "I looked back. I knew I'd cry if I did, but crying didn't scare me anymore.", "(Walk on without looking back, like Seoyun)", "I tried not to look back. Only at the gate did I give in and look back, once.", "Seoyun was still standing right where I'd left her. Holding the thirteen-year-old sign above her head. “My Dad = Character No. 1.”", "Dad! Eat! For real!", "You too! For real!", "On the plane, I ate the burst kimbap alone. The seat beside me was empty. Outside the window, clouds drifted slowly by.", "Chapter 18  Our Family", "Back in Seoul, development entered the home stretch.", "As I'd promised Jisoo, I reported every all-nighter. Then a thermos of barley tea would be delivered from the bookstore. The delivery man was Minho.", "Boss, am I a courier now? Pay me a fee.", "I'll put your name in big letters in the end credits.", "On Thursdays I still stopped by the bookstore. The readings went on, and the barley tea tasted the same. But the words I'd left on that rock by the Kamo River stayed sunk at the bottom of the teacup.", "Boss, since you got back from Kyoto, you look strange every Thursday. Did something happen?", "Nothing happened. …That's the problem.", "The last thing left was the ending. The original ends in Kyoto, but the game had to show what came after.", "How many endings should we do? The original has one, but in a game it's more fun if it branches depending on what you choose.", "How should the endings branch?", "“Let's add them. But whichever path you take, someone who was loved was loved.”", "Then what changes from ending to ending?", "How early you notice it. And who you tell. That's all.", "“…There was a time I thought my life was a bad ending.”", "And now?", "It wasn't an ending at all. Just a long Act Three.", "The game came out in the fall. In the end credits, my name went up next to “Programming,” and my daughter's next to “Original Work,” side by side.", "Comma Books went under “Barley Tea Sponsor,” and Park Minho under “Delivery.” Minho took a screenshot and made it his profile picture.", "Comma Books went under “Barley Tea Sponsor,” and Park Minho under “Investigation Committee.” Minho took a screenshot and made it his profile picture.", "Chuseok. I brought Jisoo to my parents' house for the first time.", "Mother had set the table on the porch and was waiting. With her wrist out of the cast.", "So the barley tea girl's here. Did you use up that barley?", "Yes. So I tried roasting it myself like you do, but I can't get that taste at all.", "Your flame's too high. Come here. I'll show you.", "Mother took the faded flowered apron from the wardrobe and tied it on Jisoo. The same apron I'd worn to school in the fall of Seoyun's fourth grade.", "The two of them disappeared into the kitchen. Only Father and I were left on the porch.", "Is she the one who brought you tea when you were shaking?", "Yes.", "Good. Then that's good.", "That was the whole of Father's evaluation. Then he went into his room and came out holding something.", "The drawing of hands Seoyun made fifteen years ago, and the four hands she drew before leaving the country. Taken down from the wall.", "We'll need one more hand drawn in here. Have Seoyun do it when she comes.", "A space for one more hand.", "“…Father, I haven't even proposed yet.”", "Then do it. What are you dawdling for? I proposed to your mother three months after we met.", "(from the kitchen) Without a ring! Don't copy that part!", "“Yes. Save the space for the drawing.”", "Now you finally sound like a man.", "(from the kitchen) Make sure you get a ring! Your father didn't even do that!", "The smell of roasting barley came from the kitchen. Jisoo's and Mother's laughter mingled.", "I lay down on the porch. The same spot where I'd fallen into a dreamless sleep fifteen years ago. This time I didn't fall asleep. I listened to those sounds for a long time.", "Late autumn. Sunset fell over the hill behind the neighborhood.", "In my pocket was a ring box, and a note of proposal lines Minho had written for me. Half a sheet of A4.", "(My hand gripping the ring box was sweating. The old me, the one who always got ready to be rejected first, raises his head one last time.)", "Minho's note in my pocket.", "(Throw away the memorized lines and speak honestly)", "I crumpled the note back into my pocket. If Seoyun had seen, she'd have said well done.", "(Recite the memorized lines)", "Jisoo, you are my life's, um, spring day's… spring day's…", "Jeongwoo. That's Minho's handwriting, isn't it? Not the memorized stuff. Just say it.", "Jisoo. I can't say fancy words. Instead, I'll be in the same place every day.", "On rainy days I'll tilt the umbrella, on typhoon days I'll come running, and on all-nighter days I'll report.", "Please marry me.", "For a long while Jisoo didn't answer. The sunset was reflected in her glasses.", "I have conditions.", "Yes.", "From now on, bring only one umbrella. A big one. So we can share it.", "I laughed, then cried, then dropped the ring, then spent ten minutes searching for it in the silver grass.", "That was the fanciest proposal in the world. Yes.", "Spring. A small outdoor wedding.", "There weren't many guests. The Studio Moonlight family, the bookstore regulars, the old gentleman who'd collapsed and gone to the ER, and Seoyun's homeroom teacher from sports day.", "There was no officiant. Instead the CEO gave a speech. The same man who'd asked fifteen years ago, “Is this a daycare now?”", "Han used to bring his daughter to work every Saturday. Back then, I didn't like it.", "Now I know. If I'd known that little girl would become our company's best source author, I'd have given her two boxes of snacks at a time.", "Laughter broke out. Mother took out her handkerchief, and Father looked up at the sky.", "My son is finally smiling a little.", "Stand up straight. Now you've got someone to carry things with.", "Then someone with an art bag on her shoulder comes running in through the entrance.", "Dad! I almost didn't make it! I was drawing a congratulations picture on the plane!", "Out of breath, Seoyun held out a frame. A drawing of five overlapping hands.", "Two wrinkled hands, a hand with a wrist brace, a hand smudged with charcoal. And the hand that carried books.", "Grandpa said to draw one more in. I finished it.", "It clashed with the first day of my graduation show, so I told my professor. That it's my dad's wedding. Honestly. And he said to leave the wall empty for today. With a sign: “Artist away: father's wedding.”", "…Thank you for telling me.", "We agreed to send the not-okay photos too, didn't we? Though this one's an okay photo.", "Dad, are you crying?", "No.", "Your ears are red. I'm drawing that face in the book.", "Seoyun stood in front of Jisoo. They looked at each other for a moment, then smiled at the same time.", "What should I call you now?", "Whatever's comfortable. I'm your No. 1 fan. You don't have to call me Mom. Just someone who set out one more chair is fine.", "…Then I'll decide slowly. Our family's a little slow by nature. We take after Dad.", "Seoyun took a lunch box out of her bag. When she opened the lid, it was full of kimbap. Every piece with burst sides.", "Celebration food. Made by the family motto. Burst or not, it tastes the same.", "Jisoo picked one up, ate it, and her eyes went wide.", "These… burst exactly like Jeongwoo's. They taste the same too.", "It's genetic. Oh, and I decided the title for Volume 2. <Our Family>. And you have to make the sequel to the game too, Dad.", "The photographer called out, “The groom has the biggest smile of all!” I smiled even bigger.", "Before the photographer could even tell me to smile, I was already smiling.", "In the photo, my ears are red. Seoyun is pointing at them, and Jisoo is holding an umbrella. Even though it's a clear day.", "Even in all that long time when I believed I couldn't be loved, I had been loved the whole way through.", "It just took me a little longer to notice than it takes a roll of kimbap to burst.", "Spring. A small outdoor wedding.", "There weren't many guests. The Studio Moonlight family, the bookstore regulars, the old gentleman who'd collapsed and gone to the ER, and Seoyun's homeroom teacher from sports day.", "The CEO gave a speech. The same man who'd asked fifteen years ago, “Is this a daycare now?” Laughter broke out, and Mother took out her handkerchief.", "One seat in the front row was empty. On the chair lay a yellow hairpin.", "Seoyun couldn't come. A text had come two days before. “My deadline blew up. Sorry, Dad. I'll watch on video. I'm fine!”", "“I'm fine.” Those were the words I could least stand in the world, and yet this time I didn't ask further. I figured she was busy. As always, we just traded okay-looking photos.", "As the ceremony was ending, Jisoo held up her phone. Seoyun was on the screen. White walls, hanging pictures, and badly swollen eyes.", "Dad! Auntie! Congratulations on your wedding! Is it okay if I… cry right now? It's a wedding.", "Seoyun, where are you? That's not your dorm, is it?", "Instead of answering, Seoyun turned the screen. Large paintings hanging on the walls. The banner at the entrance was in Japanese, but I could read the numbers. Today's date.", "It's my graduation show. Today's the first day. …The dates clashed.", "Why didn't you tell me?", "If I'd told you, you'd have postponed the wedding. You really would have.", "I would have. Seoyun knew me too well. And I had never once asked what lay behind her “I'm fine.”", "I wrote you a letter. Auntie, please read it. I can't read it right now.", "Jisoo took out an envelope. Seoyun had mailed it ahead of time. Jisoo's voice spread low across the garden.", "Dad. I always sent you only okay-looking photos. You did the same. We cared about each other so much that we lied to each other.", "When my graduation project fell apart three times, when I cried all night over visa papers, all I sent was “Did you eat?” And you answered “I ate.” Though neither of us had.", "So I'm glad you have Auntie now. Now there's someone you can tell when you're not okay.", "Next time I'll tell you too. I'll send the not-okay photos too. Congratulations on your wedding. I love you. Your double-serving daughter, Seoyun.", "The guests fell silent. Mother handed me her handkerchief. Father looked up at the sky.", "My daughter on the screen is crying.", "“Seoyun. There were lots of times I wasn't okay either. I'll tell you from now on.”", "…Really? It's a promise.", "A pinky touched the screen. Across the sea, on the glass, once again.", "“I'll come next week. To see your show. The two of us.”", "Really? Auntie too?", "Looks like we've just decided on our honeymoon. Kyoto.", "The photographer called out that it was his first group photo with a phone in it.", "In the photo, inside the screen, Seoyun is laughing as she cries. Beside her, my ears are red. Instead of an umbrella, Jisoo is holding a phone.", "We were happy. But folded into that happiness were still a few words we hadn't said to each other.", "Unfolding those words will take a little longer than it takes a roll of kimbap to burst.", "That winter was long.", "On Thursdays I'd go as far as the bookstore, see the barley tea steam rising behind the window, and turn back. In the end, I translated the “slowly” I'd heard by the Kamo River as “no.”", "On Thursdays I'd go as far as the bookstore, see the barley tea steam rising behind the window, and turn back. The words I couldn't say by the Kamo River held my ankles.", "Jisoo sent two texts. “Busy these days?” and “I roasted fresh barley.” I replied, “Lots of work at the office.” A conversation I'd seen somewhere before.", "From Seoyun, photos came every Sunday. The river, a window, pencil shavings. “Dad, did you eat?”", "I sent back that photo of ramen boiled in a pot. Same pot, same angle.", "We sent each other only our most okay-looking photos. And we each pretended to believe them.", "One evening in December. Sleet fell at the bus stop. Neither snow nor rain, it piled on my shoulders and melted.", "Under the shelter stood a kid in a school uniform. No umbrella. Stamping her feet, looking at her phone.", "I had an umbrella in my hand. As always, just one.", "There's only one umbrella.", "(Hand over the umbrella) “Take this. I'll be home in no time once I'm on the bus.”", "A lie. My bus was twenty minutes away. Just like at that bus stop the first time.", "(Hand over the umbrella) “…Take it. I'm fine.”", "“I'm fine” came out of my mouth far too easily. The words Seoyun hated most in the world.", "The kid bowed and ran off. I stood outside the shelter. The sleet soaked my glasses.", "Then my phone buzzed. It was Seoyun.", "(text) Dad, did you eat?", "(reply) I ate.", "I hadn't. And Seoyun probably hadn't either.", "I walked home. The nice guy got wet again today. No one held an umbrella over him. Because he never asked anyone to.", "Taking off my wet shoes at the door, I noticed the two bookmarks in my wallet. The smudged one and the unsmudged one.", "(…Is it not too late yet?)", "I couldn't answer that question. But asking it was the first thing I'd done all that long winter.", "The next spring, I went to Kyoto again. This time alone, and this time I packed two umbrellas.", "Seoyun's graduation show. In the middle of a white wall hung one large painting.", "A big hand with a wrist brace, holding a small hand. The same composition she'd drawn on the porch at seven. Only this time it filled an entire wall.", "The title card was in Korean and Japanese side by side. <The Same Hands, Sixteen Years>.", "Dad, what do you think? Be honest this time. And skip the part about not knowing how to look at art.", "In front of the painting of the big hand.", "“…It's big. Was my hand ever this big?”", "It is. Mine fits inside it completely. Even now.", "“Honestly, I don't know. But I can't take my eyes off it.”", "That's the best review there is. My professor said exactly the same thing.", "Visitors stood in front of the painting a long time. A few recognized my wrist and bowed. By now I'd gotten a little used to bowing ninety degrees.", "Out of the gallery, onto the bank of the Kamo River. Seoyun bought fish cakes from the convenience store. We sat side by side on that rock.", "Dad. Can I talk about Auntie Jisoo?", "Go ahead.", "When she said let's go slowly—are you still translating that as “no”?", "Why couldn't you say it that day? I saw everything from the bridge. Your mouth opened and closed three times.", "I could have pretended I was fine. But we were people who'd agreed to send the not-okay photos too now.", "I was scared. When something good comes, I guess I still think it must be a mistake. That habit doesn't get cured with one pinky.", "…Yeah. I know. Because you're Dad.", "And now? Are you lonely?", "Sometimes. I won't say I'm fine. Sometimes I'm lonely. But I still go to the bookstore on Thursdays. I do readings, and drink barley tea.", "If that's how it is… then it's still being serialized. Not finished.", "Seoyun hugged her knees and looked at the river. Across it, the lanterns came on one by one.", "Dad, I've decided the title for Volume 2. <The Two of Us>. You and me. Right now, that's the most accurate title.", "But I don't know about Volume 3. That's up to how you write it.", "That night in the dorm's shared kitchen, we rolled kimbap. Both burst. Naturally.", "On the plane home, I took out my phone, hesitated for a long time, and sent one text.", "(text) Jisoo. I'm coming back from Kyoto. I have something to tell you next Thursday. This time I'll say it all the way to the end.", "The reply came in three seconds. One barley tea emoji, and one line.", "(text) I'll be waiting. Bring only one umbrella. A big one.", "Clouds passed outside the window. Up above those clouds, for the first time, I looked forward to next Thursday."];
